const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 07 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 07 — The Decree Must Bite: Jolly George Varghese and Execution"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Concept and general principles of execution (Sec 36-37, 47) · Courts which execute (Sec 38-46) · Application and procedure (Sec 51-54, Order XXI Rules 1-40) · Arrest and detention (Sec 55-59) · Attachment (Sec 60-64) · Sale (Sec 65-67, Order XXI Rules 64-96) · Objections, claims and obstruction (Order XXI Rules 58, 89-92, 97-103)"));
c.push(STORY("Kochi, 1980. The Bank of Cochin holds a money decree for over two lakh rupees against Jolly George Varghese and his family. The bank moves to execute it by arresting the judgment-debtors and sending them to the civil prison. The executing court orders arrest without asking whether they have the means to pay. The matter reaches Justice Krishna Iyer, who reads Section 51 of the Code alongside Article 11 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights — 'no one shall be imprisoned merely on the ground of inability to fulfil a contractual obligation' — and Article 21 of the Constitution. The proviso to Section 51, he holds, is the Indian answer: a judgment-debtor may be detained only if the court is satisfied, after giving him an opportunity, that he is likely to abscond, or has dishonestly transferred or concealed property, or has had the means to pay since the decree and refuses or neglects to pay; mere poverty is not a ground. The arrest was set aside. The judgment is the humane face of a chapter that is otherwise about force: the decree that a court has passed must be made to bite, and Order XXI — the longest Order in the Code, with one hundred and six rules — is the machinery for doing so, by delivering property, attaching and selling it, appointing a receiver, or in the last resort arresting the person who will not obey."));
c.push(H3("What execution is — Sec 36-47"));
c.push(P("Execution is the enforcement of a decree or order by the process of the court; the decree-holder gets what the decree gave him. **Sec 36:** the provisions relating to execution of decrees apply to execution of orders. **Sec 37:** the 'court which passed the decree' includes, where that court has ceased to exist or to have jurisdiction, the court which would have jurisdiction to try the suit at the time of the application (Sec 37(b)). **Sec 38:** a decree may be executed either by the court which passed it or by the court to which it is sent for execution. **Sec 39:** transfer of the decree to another court on the application of the decree-holder where the judgment-debtor resides or carries on business or has property within that court's jurisdiction, or where the decree directs sale or delivery of immovable property outside the local limits, or for any other reason recorded; the transferee court must be one that would have jurisdiction to try the suit (Sec 39(3), 2002); a court does not execute a decree against property outside its local limits (Sec 39(4), 2002 — overriding Mohit Bhimrao Deshmukh). **Sec 40-41:** transfer to a court in another State and the certificate of execution. **Sec 42:** the transferee court has the same powers as the court which passed the decree, but it cannot order execution at the instance of a transferee of the decree, or against a legal representative not already a party, or attach a decree, or send the decree to another court (Sec 42(4), 1976 — except with the leave of the court which passed the decree). **Sec 43-45:** execution of decrees passed by courts in places to which the Code does not extend, and decrees of foreign courts of reciprocating territories (Sec 44A). **Sec 46:** precepts — a court may issue a precept to another court to attach property pending transfer, for two months. **Sec 47 — questions to be determined by the court executing the decree:** all questions arising between the parties to the suit or their representatives, and relating to the execution, discharge or satisfaction of the decree, shall be determined by the executing court and not by a separate suit; a plaintiff whose suit was dismissed and a defendant against whom a suit was dismissed are parties; a purchaser at a court sale is deemed a party; since 1976 an order under Sec 47 is not a decree and is not appealable as such, only revisable. The executing court cannot go behind the decree — it must take it as it stands and cannot question its correctness (Vasudev Dhanjibhai Modi v. Rajabhai Abdul Rehman, 1970 — but it may refuse to execute a decree that is a nullity for want of inherent jurisdiction, Kiran Singh; Rafique Bibi v. Sayed Waliuddin, 2004 — 'nullity' is confined to lack of jurisdiction over the subject-matter, not a mere error); it may construe an ambiguous decree by reading the judgment and pleadings (Bhavan Vaja v. Solanki Hanuji Khodaji Mansang, 1972). **Sec 48** (limitation of twelve years) was repealed in 1963 and now lives in Art 136 of the Limitation Act. **Sec 49-50:** transferee of a decree; legal representative liable to the extent of the estate. **Sec 51 — modes of execution:** (a) delivery of any property specifically decreed; (b) attachment and sale, or sale without attachment, of any property; (c) arrest and detention in prison for a period not exceeding that in Sec 58; (d) appointment of a receiver; (e) such other manner as the nature of the relief may require — with the proviso on arrest already stated. **Sec 52-54:** execution against a legal representative from the property of the deceased; enforcement of a decree for partition of revenue-paying estates by the Collector."));
c.push(H3("Application and procedure — Order XXI Rules 1-40"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: money under a decree is paid into court, by postal money order or bank, or directly to the decree-holder; interest ceases from the date of notice of deposit (1976); Rule 2: payment or adjustment out of court must be certified and recorded, on the application of the decree-holder or the judgment-debtor within thirty days (Art 125 Limitation), and an uncertified payment or adjustment is not recognised by the executing court (Rule 2(3)); Rule 3-9: lands in more than one jurisdiction, transfer of decrees, the certificate; **Rule 10-11:** application for execution — oral at the time of judgment for a money decree if the judgment-debtor is present, otherwise written, in the prescribed form, signed and verified, stating the number of the suit, the parties, the date of the decree, previous applications, the amount due, the mode of assistance required; Rule 11A: a written application for arrest must state the grounds; Rule 12-13: applications for attachment of movables not in the judgment-debtor's possession and of immovable property must contain a description; Rule 14: the court may require the decree-holder to produce a certified extract of the register; Rule 15: by whom application is made (joint decree-holders); Rule 16: transferee of the decree may apply with notice to the transferor and the judgment-debtor; **Rule 17:** the court examines whether the requirements of Rules 11-14 are complied with and may allow the defect to be remedied then and there — the application is not rejected for a curable defect (the 1976 amendment); Rule 18-19: cross-decrees and cross-claims are set off; Rule 20: decree for sale of mortgaged property; **Rule 21:** simultaneous execution against person and property is at the court's discretion (Shyam Singh v. Collector, 1993 — ordinarily not both at once); **Rule 22 — notice to show cause:** where the application is made more than two years after the decree, or against a legal representative, or against the assignee of an insolvent, or for execution of a decree filed under Sec 44A (1976 — and, by the same amendment, where the application is against a person adjudged insolvent), the court shall issue notice to the person against whom execution is applied for; the court may dispense with notice if it would cause unreasonable delay or defeat the ends of justice, recording reasons; Rule 23: after hearing, the court orders execution, or if the person does not appear or does not show cause, orders execution forthwith; Rule 24: process for execution is issued, and Rule 25: endorsement of the day of execution; Rule 26: the executing court may stay execution for a reasonable time to allow the judgment-debtor to apply to the court which passed the decree, or to the appellate court, for stay; Rule 27-29: restitution of stay, and stay pending a suit between the parties; **Rule 30-36:** decree for money — by detention, attachment, or both; for specific movable property — by seizure and delivery, detention or attachment; for specific performance, restitution of conjugal rights or an injunction — by detention or attachment (except that a decree for restitution of conjugal rights is enforced only by attachment, Rule 32(1) proviso, and Rule 33 allows the court to order periodical payments instead); execution of decree for the execution of a document or endorsement of a negotiable instrument — the court executes the document if the judgment-debtor refuses (Rule 34); decree for immovable property — delivery of possession, breaking open doors if necessary, and symbolical delivery where the property is in the occupancy of a tenant (Rule 35-36); **Rule 37-40 — arrest and detention:** the court shall, instead of issuing a warrant, issue a notice calling upon the judgment-debtor to appear and show cause why he should not be committed to civil prison (Rule 37 — the notice may be dispensed with if he is likely to abscond); Rule 38: the warrant of arrest; **Rule 39:** subsistence allowance must be paid by the decree-holder; **Rule 40:** the proceedings on appearance — the court hears the decree-holder and the judgment-debtor, takes evidence, and may commit him only after finding one of the Sec 51 grounds, or release him on security, or on his undertaking to apply for insolvency."));
c.push(H3("Arrest and detention — Sec 55-59"));
c.push(P("**Sec 55:** a judgment-debtor may be arrested in execution at any hour and on any day and brought before the court as soon as practicable, and detained in civil prison; no dwelling-house is entered after sunset or before sunrise; no outer door is broken open unless the house is in his occupancy and he refuses; a room in the actual occupancy of a woman who by custom does not appear in public is not entered until she has been given an opportunity to withdraw; a judgment-debtor who pays the amount and costs to the arresting officer is released at once; where the decree is for money and the judgment-debtor declares to the court that he intends to apply to be declared an insolvent, the court may release him on security for one month (Sec 55(4)). **Sec 56:** no woman is arrested or detained in execution of a decree for the payment of money. **Sec 57:** subsistence allowance is fixed by the State Government or the court. **Sec 58 — detention and release:** for decrees above five thousand rupees, up to three months; for decrees between two thousand and five thousand rupees, up to six weeks (1999 figures); below two thousand rupees, no detention; the judgment-debtor is released on payment, on the decree-holder's request, on his omission to pay subsistence allowance, or on the decree being satisfied, and once released he cannot be re-arrested under the same decree; release does not discharge the debt (Sec 58(2)). **Sec 59:** release on the ground of serious illness; the court may cancel the warrant if he is not in a fit state of health, and a person released on that ground may be re-arrested. The constitutional limit is the Sec 51 proviso as explained in **Jolly George Varghese**: the executing court must record a finding of means and wilful refusal, or of dishonest transfer, or of a likelihood of absconding; to imprison a poor debtor is to violate Art 21."));
c.push(H3("Attachment — Sec 60-64 and Order XXI Rules 41-57"));
c.push(P("**Sec 60(1):** property liable to attachment and sale — lands, houses, goods, money, bank notes, cheques, promissory notes, government securities, bonds, debts, shares in a corporation, and all other saleable property, movable or immovable, belonging to the judgment-debtor or over which he has a disposing power he may exercise for his own benefit, whether held in his name or by another in trust for him. The **exemptions** in the proviso: (a) necessary wearing apparel, cooking vessels, beds and bedding of the judgment-debtor, his wife and children, and personal ornaments a woman cannot part with by religious usage; (b) tools of artisans and, for an agriculturist, implements of husbandry, cattle and seed-grain needed to earn a livelihood, and land; (c) houses and buildings belonging to an agriculturist, a labourer or a domestic servant and occupied by him; (d) books of account; (e) a mere right to sue for damages; (f) any right of personal service; (g) stipends and gratuities of pensioners and political pensions; (h) wages of labourers and domestic servants; (i) salary to the extent of the first one thousand rupees and two-thirds of the remainder (1999), and in execution of a maintenance decree the first one thousand rupees only; (ia) one-third of the salary in execution of a maintenance decree; (j) pay of persons under the Air Force, Army and Navy Acts; (k) compulsory deposits and other sums in the Provident Funds Act, and gratuity; (ka) deposits under the Public Provident Fund Act; (kb) money payable under a life insurance policy; (kc) the interest of a lessee of a residential building under a rent control law; (l) any allowance forming part of the emoluments declared exempt by the Government; (m) an expectancy of succession; (n) a right to future maintenance; (o) allowances declared exempt; (p) property exempt by any other law. An agreement waiving the exemption is void (Sec 60(1A), 1976). **Sec 61:** the State Government may exempt a portion of agricultural produce. **Sec 62:** seizure of property in a dwelling-house — the officer does not enter after sunset or before sunrise, does not break open the outer door, and gives a pardanashin woman an opportunity to withdraw. **Sec 63:** where property is attached by more than one court, the court of the highest grade, or, if equal, the court which first attached, receives and realises the property. **Sec 64:** private alienation of property after attachment is void as against all claims enforceable under the attachment — but (2002) not a transfer made in pursuance of a contract entered into and registered before the attachment (Vannarakkal Kallalathil Sreedharan v. Chandramaath Balakrishnan, 1990 — an agreement of sale before attachment prevails over the attachment). **Order XXI Rules 41-57:** examination of the judgment-debtor as to property, and an affidavit of assets (Rule 41, 1976 — the court may order the judgment-debtor to file an affidavit of his assets and may detain him for three months for failure to do so); attachment of movable property in possession of the judgment-debtor by actual seizure (Rule 43); agricultural produce (Rule 44-45); **debts, shares and other property not in possession** — by a prohibitory order (Rule 46); **garnishee proceedings** (Rule 46A-46I, 1976) — the court may serve notice on the garnishee (the judgment-debtor's debtor) to pay into court, and on his failure to dispute may execute against him; shares (Rule 47); **salary** (Rule 48-48A — attachment of salary of public officers and private employees by an order to the disbursing officer, subject to Sec 60); partnership property (Rule 49); property of the deceased and of partners (Rule 50); negotiable instruments (Rule 51); property in the custody of a court or public officer (Rule 52); attachment of a decree (Rule 53); **immovable property** by a prohibitory order affixed on the property and at the court-house and Collector's office, and proclaimed by beat of drum (Rule 54); determination of attachment on satisfaction (Rule 55); Rule 57 (1976): where the attachment ceases on the dismissal of the application the court must state whether it continues, otherwise it ceases. **Rule 58 — claims and objections to attachment:** where a claim is preferred or an objection made to the attachment of any property on the ground that the property is not liable to attachment, the court shall proceed to adjudicate the claim — except where the property has already been sold, or the court considers the claim designedly or unnecessarily delayed; the adjudication has the force of a decree and is appealable (Rule 58(4), 1976 — no separate suit); the claimant must show that on the date of attachment he was in possession on his own account or under a title of his own (Rule 58(2) refers to all questions including title); Rule 59: stay of sale; Rule 60-63 were replaced by the 1976 scheme. Rule 46A-46I garnishee orders are a complete code (Bank of Baroda v. Motibhai, 1994)."));
c.push(H3("Sale — Sec 65-67 and Order XXI Rules 64-96"));
c.push(P("**Sec 65:** where immovable property is sold in execution and the sale has become absolute, the property vests in the purchaser from the time it is sold, not from the confirmation — relation back. **Sec 66** (repealed in 2002; earlier prohibited suits against certified purchasers on the ground that the purchase was benami — now Benami Transactions (Prohibition) Act). **Sec 67:** the State Government may make rules for the sale of land in execution for arrears of land revenue. **Order XXI Rules 64-66:** the court may order any property attached to be sold and the proceeds paid to the decree-holder — but only so much as is necessary to satisfy the decree (Rule 64; Ambati Narasayya v. M. Subba Rao, 1989 — sale of the whole property when a part would suffice is illegal; Takkaseela Pedda Subba Reddi v. Pujari Padmavathamma, 1977); sales are conducted by an officer of the court by public auction (Rule 65); **proclamation of sale** (Rule 66) — after notice to the decree-holder and judgment-debtor, stating the property, the encumbrances, the amount, and every other thing the court considers material for a purchaser to know, including the estimated value given by each party (1976 — the court is not required to state its own valuation, Desh Bandhu Gupta v. N.L. Anand, 1994); Rule 67-68: mode of proclamation and time of sale — not before fifteen days for immovable property and seven days for movables from the proclamation; Rule 69: adjournment or stoppage of sale — a sale adjourned for more than thirty days requires a fresh proclamation; Rule 71: defaulting purchaser answerable for loss on resale; Rule 72: the decree-holder may bid only with the express permission of the court, and a purchase without permission may be set aside on the judgment-debtor's application; Rule 72A: a mortgagee may not bid without leave; Rule 73: officers of the court may not bid. **Movable property (Rules 74-81):** sale of agricultural produce, growing crops, negotiable instruments and shares; delivery to the purchaser; the sale is complete on payment and is not set aside for irregularity, though a suit for compensation lies (Rule 78). **Immovable property (Rules 82-96):** the sale is conducted by the court or its officer; the purchaser deposits twenty-five per cent immediately and the balance within fifteen days (Rules 84-85 — a mandatory requirement; failure means the sale is a nullity and there is no need for an application to set it aside, Manilal Mohanlal Shah v. Sardar Sayed Ahmed, 1954; Balram v. Ilam Singh, 1996); resale on default (Rule 86); bid of a co-sharer preferred (Rule 88); **Rule 89 — application to set aside sale on deposit:** any person owning the property or holding an interest in it may apply to set aside the sale on depositing in court five per cent of the purchase money for the purchaser and the amount specified in the proclamation for recovery from the judgment-debtor, within sixty days of the sale (Art 127 Limitation, as amended in 2002); **Rule 90 — application to set aside on the ground of irregularity or fraud:** the decree-holder, the purchaser, or any person whose interests are affected may apply on the ground of a **material irregularity or fraud in publishing or conducting the sale** — but no sale shall be set aside unless the applicant proves that he has sustained **substantial injury** by reason of the irregularity or fraud, and no application is entertained on a ground which the applicant could have taken before the sale (1976); Rule 91: the purchaser may apply to set aside the sale on the ground that the judgment-debtor had no saleable interest; Rule 92: where no application is made or it is disallowed the court makes an order **confirming** the sale, and it becomes absolute; where the application is allowed the sale is set aside and the purchaser's money is returned; no suit lies to set aside an order under this rule; Rule 93: return of purchase money; Rule 94: certificate of sale; Rule 95-96: delivery of possession to the purchaser — actual delivery where the property is in the occupancy of the judgment-debtor, symbolical delivery where in the occupancy of a tenant. **Rules 97-103 — resistance and obstruction (1976):** where the decree-holder or purchaser is resisted or obstructed in obtaining possession, he applies to the court complaining of the resistance (Rule 97); the court adjudicates upon the application and, if satisfied that the resistance was by the judgment-debtor or a person at his instigation, directs the applicant to be put in possession and may detain the obstructor for thirty days (Rule 98); where a person other than the judgment-debtor is dispossessed by the decree-holder or purchaser he may apply complaining of the dispossession (Rule 99); the court adjudicates (Rule 100); **all questions including questions of title arising between the parties to the application are determined by the executing court and not by a separate suit (Rule 101)**; Rule 102: nothing in Rules 98 and 100 applies to a transferee pendente lite; Rule 103: orders under Rules 98 and 100 have the force of a decree. This scheme means a stranger claiming an independent right must be heard in execution itself (Brahmdeo Chaudhary v. Rishikesh Prasad Jaiswal, 1997; Shreenath v. Rajesh, 1998 — a third party may raise his objection before dispossession; Silverline Forum v. Rajiv Trust, 1998). **Rule 105-106 (1976):** hearing of applications and setting aside orders passed ex parte in execution."));
c.push(CASE("Jolly George Varghese v. Bank of Cochin (1980) 2 SCC 360","Arrest and detention in execution of a money decree requires a finding under the proviso to Sec 51 of means and wilful refusal, dishonest transfer, or likelihood of absconding; imprisoning a debtor for mere inability to pay violates Art 21 and Art 11 ICCPR."));
c.push(CASE("Vasudev Dhanjibhai Modi v. Rajabhai Abdul Rehman (1970) 1 SCC 670","The executing court cannot go behind the decree or question its validity except where the decree is a nullity for want of inherent jurisdiction."));
c.push(CASE("Manilal Mohanlal Shah v. Sardar Sayed Ahmed Sayed Mahamad AIR 1954 SC 349","Order XXI Rules 84-85 are mandatory; a sale in which the full purchase money is not deposited within fifteen days is a nullity and the court has no discretion to extend time."));
c.push(CASE("Ambati Narasayya v. M. Subba Rao 1989 Supp (2) SCC 693","Order XXI Rule 64 permits sale only of so much property as is necessary to satisfy the decree; sale of the entire property when a portion would suffice vitiates the sale."));
c.push(CASE("Silverline Forum Pvt. Ltd. v. Rajiv Trust (1998) 3 SCC 723","Under Order XXI Rules 97-103 the executing court must adjudicate the claim of a third party who resists delivery of possession, including questions of title; a separate suit is barred."));
c.push(DIG("Execution has become the digital bottleneck of civil justice: the e-Courts case management system tracks execution petitions separately after the Supreme Court's 2021 directions in Rahul S. Shah v. Jinendra Kumar Gandhi (execution to be completed within six months, the executing court to record the judgment-debtor's assets, and the decree court to pass a complete decree with all necessary directions). Attachment of bank accounts is by electronic prohibitory orders served on the bank under Order XXI Rule 46, and garnishee proceedings apply to UPI and wallet balances; attachment of salary is served on the employer's payroll system; attachment of immovable property is noted in the Dharani and Webland digital land records; court auctions of attached property are conducted on e-auction platforms with the Rule 66 proclamation published online."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Sec 47', 'Precept', 'Garnishee order', 'Property exempt from attachment', 'Setting aside a court sale', 'Symbolical delivery', 'Rule 97-103'. Part B: 'Explain the modes of execution of a decree under Sec 51 and the circumstances in which a judgment-debtor may be arrested and detained' — Sec 51 with the proviso, Jolly George Varghese, Sec 55-59, Order XXI Rules 37-40; or 'Discuss the procedure for attachment and sale of immovable property in execution and the remedies against an irregular sale' — Rule 54, 64-66, 84-85, 89-92, cases. Part C: a judgment-debtor's salary is attached in full — Sec 60(1)(i) limits; a purchaser fails to deposit the balance within fifteen days — sale is a nullity (Manilal); a stranger in possession is dispossessed by the purchaser — Rule 99-101 application, no suit."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 08 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 08 — Suing the Sarkar: Geeta Iron and Brass Works and the Special Suits"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Suits by or against the Government and public officers (Sec 79-82, Order XXVII) · The notice under Sec 80 · Suits by aliens and by or against foreign rulers, ambassadors and rulers of former Indian States (Sec 83-87B) · Suits relating to public nuisances and public charities (Sec 91-93) · Interpleader suits (Sec 88, Order XXXV) · Incidental and supplemental proceedings (Sec 75-78, 94-95)"));
c.push(STORY("Punjab, 1978. Geeta Iron and Brass Works has a contract with the State for the supply of goods; a dispute arises and the firm sues the State for the price. The State of Punjab takes every technical point it can find — the notice under Section 80 was defective, the suit was premature, the description of the plaintiff was wrong. The Supreme Court, through Justice Krishna Iyer, dismissed the State's appeal and delivered a rebuke that governments have been quoting against themselves ever since: the State is a litigant 'in a category apart', it should not 'fight legal battles on technical grounds', and Section 80 exists so that the Government may settle a just claim without litigation, not so that it may defeat a just claim after litigation. 'The State should be a model litigant.' Two years earlier Parliament had amended Section 80 to let a court dispense with notice in urgent cases and to bar the dismissal of a suit for a mere defect in the notice. This episode is about the special rules that apply when one of the parties is the Government, a foreign State, or the public itself — and about the machinery of commissions and supplemental orders that the court uses in any suit to see what it cannot see from the bench."));
c.push(H3("Suits by or against the Government — Sec 79-82, Order XXVII"));
c.push(P("**Sec 79:** in a suit by or against the Government the authority named as plaintiff or defendant is the Union of India or the State (Art 300 of the Constitution). **Sec 80 — notice:** no suit shall be instituted against the Government or against a public officer in respect of any act purporting to be done by him in his official capacity until the expiration of **two months** after a notice in writing has been delivered to, or left at the office of, the Secretary to the Government (or the Collector of the district for a State, or the General Manager of a railway) and, in the case of a public officer, delivered to him or left at his office, **stating the cause of action, the name, description and place of residence of the plaintiff, and the relief he claims**; the plaint must contain a statement that the notice was so delivered. The 1976 amendment added **Sec 80(2):** a suit to obtain an urgent or immediate relief may be instituted with the leave of the court without serving notice, but the court shall not grant relief, interim or otherwise, except after giving the Government a reasonable opportunity of showing cause, and shall return the plaint for presentation after the notice if it finds no urgent relief need be granted; and **Sec 80(3):** no suit shall be dismissed merely by reason of any error or defect in the notice if the name, description and residence of the plaintiff, the cause of action and the relief are substantially identifiable. The purpose is to give the Government an opportunity to reconsider its position and settle (Bihari Chowdhary v. State of Bihar, 1984 — the notice is mandatory and a suit filed before the two months is liable to be dismissed; the Constitution Bench in State of Punjab v. Geeta Iron and Brass Works, 1978, and later Raghunath Das v. Union of India, 1969, held that the notice must be construed reasonably, not pedantically, and that substantial compliance is enough; State of A.P. v. Pioneer Builders, 2006 — the notice under Sec 80(1) and the leave under 80(2) are mandatory; a suit without either cannot proceed; Ghanshyam Dass v. Dominion of India, 1984 — the identity of the plaintiff and the cause of action are what matter; Amar Nath Dogra v. Union of India, 1963 — the Government may waive the notice). The Law Commission's 14th Report recommended abolition of Sec 80; Parliament kept it with the 1976 safeguards. Notice is not required for a suit against a public officer for an act not purporting to be done in official capacity, nor for a writ petition, nor for an application under the Arbitration Act. **Sec 81:** exemption of a public officer from arrest and from personal appearance when it would interfere with public duty. **Sec 82:** where a decree is passed against the Union of India, a State or a public officer in respect of an official act, execution shall not be issued unless the decree remains unsatisfied for three months from its date (1976 — the earlier requirement of a report to the Government was replaced). **Order XXVII:** plaints and written statements are signed by a person authorised by the Government (Rule 1); recognised agents (Rule 2); the plaint states the name of the Government instead of a person (Rule 3); the Government pleader is the agent for service (Rule 4); the court fixes the day for appearance so as to allow the Government time to communicate with the officers (Rule 5 — a reasonable time); Rule 5A (1976): the Government is joined as a party in a suit against a public officer for an official act; Rule 5B (1976): the court's duty to assist in arriving at a settlement in every suit against the Government — the first place the Code mentions settlement; Rule 6: attendance of a person able to answer questions; Rule 7: extension of time to enable a public officer to make a reference to the Government; Rule 8: procedure in suits against public officers; Rule 8A: no security is required from the Government; Rule 8B: definitions. Order XXVII-A: suits involving a substantial question of law as to the interpretation of the Constitution — notice to the Attorney-General or Advocate-General."));
c.push(H3("Aliens, foreign rulers, ambassadors and rulers of former Indian States — Sec 83-87B"));
c.push(P("**Sec 83:** alien enemies residing in India with the permission of the Central Government, and alien friends, may sue in any court as if they were citizens; alien enemies residing without permission, or residing in a foreign country, shall not sue — an 'alien enemy' includes a person residing in a country at war with India or carrying on business there. **Sec 84:** a foreign State may sue in any court of India, provided the object is to enforce a private right vested in the ruler or in an officer in his public capacity. **Sec 85:** the Central Government may appoint persons to prosecute or defend suits on behalf of foreign rulers. **Sec 86 — suits against foreign rulers, ambassadors and envoys:** no foreign State may be sued in any court except with the consent of the Central Government certified in writing by a Secretary to that Government; consent may be given with respect to a specified suit or class of suits and may specify the court; and no consent is given unless it appears that the foreign State has instituted a suit against the person desiring to sue, or by itself or another trades within the local limits, or is in possession of immovable property situate within those limits and is to be sued with reference to it or for money charged on it, or has waived the privilege; the same protection extends to ambassadors and envoys (Sec 86(4)); no decree against a foreign State is executed except with the consent of the Central Government (Sec 86(3)); and no arrest of a ruler, ambassador or envoy (Sec 86(2)). The consent requirement is a codification of sovereign immunity — but restrictive: Mirza Ali Akbar Kashani v. United Arab Republic (1966) held that Sec 86 is exhaustive, so a foreign State's commercial transactions can be the subject of a suit once consent is given, and the Central Government must apply its mind and cannot refuse consent arbitrarily (Harbhajan Singh Dhalla v. Union of India, 1987 — reasons must be given; Veb Deutfracht Seereederei Rostock v. New Central Jute Mills, 1994 — a State-owned shipping company of a foreign State is a foreign State for Sec 86, and consent is needed; Ethiopian Airlines v. Ganesh Narain Saboo, 2011 — a foreign State's commercial corporation is not entitled to immunity under Sec 86 in a consumer forum, and immunity is restrictive for commercial acts). **Sec 87:** the style of foreign rulers as parties. **Sec 87A-87B:** definitions, and the application of Sec 85 and 86 to rulers of former Indian States for acts done before the commencement of the Constitution — a provision of diminishing importance after the 26th Amendment abolished privy purses and recognition of rulers (Mohan Lal Jain v. Sawai Man Singhji, 1962 — the privilege attaches to the ruler, not to his private business, and continues for pre-Constitution acts only)."));
c.push(H3("Public nuisances and public charities — Sec 91-93"));
c.push(P("**Sec 91:** in the case of a public nuisance or other wrongful act affecting or likely to affect the public, a suit for a declaration and injunction or for such other relief as may be appropriate may be instituted by the Advocate-General, or **with the leave of the court by two or more persons**, even though no special damage has been caused to such persons by reason of the nuisance (1976 — earlier the Advocate-General's consent was required; the amendment enabled public-spirited citizens to sue and is the civil-law cousin of public interest litigation); the section does not limit the right of suit that may exist independently (Sec 91(2)) — a person who has suffered special damage may sue in his own right. **Sec 92 — public charities:** in the case of any alleged breach of an express or constructive trust created for public purposes of a charitable or religious nature, or where the direction of the court is necessary for the administration of such trust, the Advocate-General, or two or more persons having an interest in the trust with the leave of the court, may institute a suit in the principal civil court of original jurisdiction or a court empowered by the State Government to obtain a decree removing a trustee, appointing a new trustee, vesting property in a trustee, directing a removed trustee to deliver possession, directing accounts and inquiries, declaring what proportion of the trust property is to be allocated to any object, authorising the whole or part of the property to be let, sold, mortgaged or exchanged, settling a **scheme**, or granting such further relief as the nature of the case may require; no suit claiming these reliefs may be instituted except in conformity with Sec 92 (Sec 92(2)); the court may alter the original purposes and allow the property to be applied cy pres where they cannot be carried out or have been adequately provided for (Sec 92(3), 1976). Conditions: a trust for public purposes of a charitable or religious nature, an alleged breach or the need for directions, and the suit must be in a representative capacity — a suit by a person to enforce his private right in the trust property is not a Sec 92 suit (Swami Parmatmanand Saraswati v. Ramji Tripathi, 1974 — the plaintiff must sue on behalf of the public, not to vindicate his own right; Vidyodaya Trust v. Mohan Prasad, 2008; Bishwanath v. Shri Thakur Radhaballabhji, 1967 — a worshipper may sue as next friend of the idol without Sec 92 where the trustee is alienating the idol's property). **Sec 93:** the exercise of powers of the Advocate-General outside the Presidency towns by the Collector or another officer appointed by the State Government."));
c.push(H3("Interpleader — Sec 88 and Order XXXV"));
c.push(P("Where two or more persons claim adversely to one another the same debt, sum of money or other property, movable or immovable, from another person who claims no interest in it other than for charges or costs and who is ready to pay or deliver it to the rightful claimant, that other person may institute a suit of interpleader against all the claimants for the purpose of obtaining a decision as to whom the payment or delivery shall be made and of obtaining indemnity for himself — provided that no such suit lies where a suit is pending in which the rights of all parties can properly be decided (Sec 88). **Order XXXV:** Rule 1: the plaint states that the plaintiff claims no interest other than charges or costs, that the claims are made by the defendants severally, and that there is no collusion; Rule 2: where the thing is capable of being paid into court the plaintiff may be required to do so; Rule 3: the court may stay proceedings in another suit by one claimant against the plaintiff; Rule 4: at the first hearing the court may declare the plaintiff discharged from all liability, award him costs and dismiss him from the suit, or retain him till final disposal, and may adjudicate the title on the spot or frame issues and try them, and may direct one of the claimants to be made plaintiff in lieu of the original; Rule 5: an agent or tenant cannot institute an interpleader suit against his principal or landlord for the purpose of compelling them to interplead with persons other than those claiming through the principal or landlord — a tenant cannot dispute his landlord's title (Sec 116 Evidence Act); Rule 6: costs. Typical examples: a bank holding a deposit claimed by rival heirs; an insurer facing two nominees; a tenant faced with two persons each claiming to be the landlord (National Insurance Co. v. Dhirendra Nath Banerjee? — the classic Indian illustrations are in Mulla). The suit does not decide the plaintiff's own liability, which he admits; it decides between the claimants."));
c.push(H3("Incidental and supplemental proceedings — Sec 75-78, 94-95"));
c.push(P("**Sec 75:** the court may issue a **commission** to examine any person, to make a local investigation, to examine or adjust accounts, to make a partition, to hold a scientific, technical or expert investigation (1976), to conduct the sale of property which is subject to speedy and natural decay (1976), and to perform any ministerial act (1976) — the detailed rules are in Order XXVI (Episode 09). **Sec 76:** a commission for the examination of a witness may be issued to any court in another State. **Sec 77:** a letter of request to examine a witness residing outside India. **Sec 78:** commissions issued by foreign courts, courts outside India established by the Central Government, and courts of the Supreme Court may be executed by Indian courts. **Sec 94 — supplemental proceedings:** in order to prevent the ends of justice from being defeated the court may, if so prescribed, (a) issue a warrant to arrest the defendant and bring him before the court to show cause why he should not give security for his appearance, and commit him to civil prison in default (Order XXXVIII Rules 1-4); (b) direct the defendant to furnish security to produce any property and, in default, order attachment (Order XXXVIII Rules 5-13); (c) grant a temporary injunction and, in case of disobedience, commit the person to civil prison and order attachment and sale of his property (Order XXXIX); (d) appoint a receiver of any property and enforce his duties by attaching and selling his property (Order XL); (e) make such other interlocutory orders as may appear to the court to be just and convenient. Sec 94 is the enabling section; the words 'if so prescribed' link it to the Orders, though the Supreme Court has held that Sec 94(e) and Sec 151 together allow interlocutory orders not specifically covered (Manohar Lal Chopra). **Sec 95 — compensation for obtaining arrest, attachment or injunction on insufficient grounds:** where an arrest, attachment before judgment or temporary injunction was applied for on insufficient grounds, or the suit fails and there was no reasonable or probable ground for instituting it, the defendant may apply and the court may award compensation up to fifty thousand rupees (1999), not exceeding the court's pecuniary limits; an order under Sec 95 bars a suit for compensation in respect of the same arrest, attachment or injunction (Sec 95(2)); the application must be made in the same suit — the remedy is summary and the defendant may instead sue for malicious prosecution. Bank of India v. Lekhimoni Das (2000): a claim for damages for wrongful attachment before judgment lies under Sec 95 in the suit itself, and a separate suit is barred to the extent of the Sec 95 limits."));
c.push(CASE("State of Punjab v. Geeta Iron and Brass Works Ltd. (1978) 1 SCC 68","The Government should be a model litigant and not defeat just claims on technical objections; Sec 80 is meant to promote settlement; the notice is to be read liberally."));
c.push(CASE("Bihari Chowdhary v. State of Bihar (1984) 2 SCC 627","Sec 80 notice is mandatory and imperative; a suit instituted before the expiry of two months is not maintainable, and the defect cannot be cured by the passage of time during the pendency of the suit."));
c.push(CASE("State of A.P. v. Pioneer Builders (2006) 12 SCC 119","A suit against the Government without notice under Sec 80(1) and without leave under Sec 80(2) is bad; leave to dispense with notice must be expressly sought and granted before the suit proceeds; the court must return the plaint if no urgent relief is warranted."));
c.push(CASE("Ethiopian Airlines v. Ganesh Narain Saboo (2011) 8 SCC 539","A State-owned foreign corporation engaged in commercial activity is not entitled to sovereign immunity; Sec 86 consent is not required for proceedings under the Consumer Protection Act; the restrictive theory of immunity applies."));
c.push(CASE("Swami Parmatmanand Saraswati v. Ramji Tripathi (1974) 2 SCC 695","A suit is under Sec 92 only if it is brought in a representative capacity for the benefit of the public in respect of a public charitable or religious trust; a suit to vindicate the plaintiff's individual right, even if it concerns a trust, is not."));
c.push(DIG("Sec 80 notices are now served on Government e-mail addresses notified under the e-Office rules, and several States accept the notice through their litigation-management portals (LIMBS for the Union — the Legal Information Management and Briefing System — which tracks every notice and suit against the Government); the two-month period runs from delivery, so proof of e-mail delivery matters. Public-nuisance suits under Sec 91 are the civil counterpart of environmental PIL, and courts admit satellite imagery and pollution-control-board data under Order XXVI commissions. Suits against foreign States over online contracts — an airline's website, a foreign university's admission portal — raise Sec 86 questions that Ethiopian Airlines answers: commercial activity is not immune."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Sec 80 notice', 'Alien enemy', 'Interpleader suit', 'Sec 92 suit', 'Suits under Sec 91', 'Sec 95 compensation'. Part B: 'Discuss the object, contents and consequences of a notice under Sec 80 with reference to the 1976 amendments and case law' — text, Bihari Chowdhary, Geeta Iron, Sec 80(2)-(3), Pioneer Builders, Order XXVII Rule 5B; or 'When can a foreign State be sued in India? Discuss Sec 86 and the doctrine of sovereign immunity'. Part C: a contractor sues the State for urgent injunction against blacklisting without notice — Sec 80(2) leave required; a tenant files an interpleader suit against his landlord and a stranger — barred by Order XXXV Rule 5; three residents sue to remove a factory's smoke — Sec 91 with leave, or in their own right if special damage."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 09 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 09 — The Court's Eyes and Ears: Bandhua Mukti Morcha and the Commission"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Commissions (Sec 75-78, Order XXVI) · Socio-legal commissions of inquiry in social-action and public interest litigation · Suits by or against minors and persons of unsound mind (Order XXXII) · Suits by indigent persons (Order XXXIII) · Death, marriage and insolvency of parties (Order XXII) · Withdrawal and compromise (Order XXIII)"));
c.push(STORY("Faridabad, Haryana, 1982. Bandhua Mukti Morcha, an organisation working against bonded labour, writes a letter to the Supreme Court about stone-quarry workers held in debt bondage near Delhi — men, women and children working in dust, living in hovels, paid nothing after 'advances' are deducted. The Court treats the letter as a writ petition and, because it cannot go to the quarries itself, does what the Code of Civil Procedure has always allowed a civil court to do: it appoints commissioners — two advocates and a social scientist — to visit the quarries, interview the workers and report. The State of Haryana objects that the commissioners' reports are not evidence, that the workers were not cross-examined, and that a court cannot decide a case on what an outsider says he saw. Justice Bhagwati's answer in 1984 created the socio-legal commission: in public interest litigation the court may appoint a commission to gather facts and the report is prima facie evidence, the parties may file affidavits in reply, and the ordinary adversarial rules of proof bend to the reality that the poor cannot themselves produce evidence against the powerful. The Court found bonded labour, ordered the workers released and rehabilitated, and directed the State to enforce the Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act. Order XXVI is the Code's machinery for the court to see through other eyes, and this episode pairs it with the other rules for litigants who cannot act for themselves — minors, persons of unsound mind, the indigent — and with what happens when a party dies, withdraws or settles."));
c.push(H3("Commissions — Order XXVI"));
c.push(P("**To examine witnesses (Rules 1-8):** the court may issue a commission for the examination on interrogatories or otherwise of any person resident within its jurisdiction who is exempted from attending court, or who is unable to attend from sickness or infirmity, or (1976) who is a Government servant unable to attend without detriment to the public service (Rule 1); the court may issue a commission for the examination of a person residing outside its jurisdiction (Rule 4), or of a person about to leave the jurisdiction, or a person in the service of the Government who cannot attend; a commission to examine a witness abroad is issued as a letter of request (Rule 5); Rule 4A (1999): the court may issue a commission for the examination of any person resident within the local limits of its jurisdiction, in the interest of justice or for the expeditious disposal of the case — the rule that, with Order XVIII Rule 4(2), allows cross-examination before a commissioner; the commissioner examines the witness, records the deposition, and returns it with the commission (Rule 7); the evidence taken under a commission is not read as evidence in the suit without the consent of the party against whom it is offered unless the person who gave it is beyond the jurisdiction, dead, or unable to attend, or the court dispenses with proof (Rule 8). **Local investigation (Rules 9-10):** the court may issue a commission to a person to make a local investigation and report on any matter in dispute, for the purpose of elucidating any matter in dispute or ascertaining the market value of property, or the amount of mesne profits, damages or annual net profits — the commissioner reduces to writing the evidence taken and returns it with his report; the report and evidence form part of the record, but the court or any party may examine the commissioner personally in open court (Rule 10(2)); where the court is dissatisfied it may order further inquiry (Rule 10(3)). The report is evidence, but the commissioner cannot be appointed to collect evidence for a party — the court appoints him to elucidate what is on record, e.g. to find out the boundaries, the state of a building, the existence of a wall; he cannot decide the case (Padam Sen v. State of U.P.; Haryana Waqf Board v. Shanti Sarup, 2008 — a commissioner's report on the physical features of the property is admissible but not on a question of title). **Scientific investigation, performance of ministerial acts, and sale of perishable property (Rules 10A-10C, 1976):** the court may issue a commission for a scientific, technical or expert investigation which cannot conveniently be conducted before the court (e.g. a chemical analysis, a survey, a DNA test), for the performance of a ministerial act (e.g. preparation of accounts, measurement), and for the sale of movable property in the custody of the court that is subject to speedy and natural decay. **Accounts (Rules 11-12):** the commissioner examines or adjusts accounts and reports; the court gives him instructions and the report is evidence, subject to the court's own examination. **Partition (Rules 13-14):** where a preliminary decree for partition has been passed, the court may issue a commission to make the partition according to the rights declared; the commissioner divides the property, allots shares, awards sums for equality of partition, and reports; the court confirms, varies or sets aside the report and passes the final decree. **General provisions (Rules 15-22):** the commissioner's expenses are deposited by the party; the commissioner may examine parties and witnesses, call for documents, and enter upon land (Rule 16-16A); the parties appear before him (Rule 17-18); a commission issued to a court in another State (Rule 19-22) — the court to which it is sent executes it and returns it. **Socio-legal commissions:** Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India (1984) held that in a proceeding under Art 32 the Supreme Court may appoint a commission to inquire and report, that the report is prima facie evidence which the parties may controvert by affidavit, and that Order XXVI's requirement of cross-examination need not be followed; the same practice applies in High Courts under Art 226 and, by Sec 151 and Order XXVI, in civil suits with a public element. Examples: Sheela Barse v. State of Maharashtra (1983 — a law professor's report on women in police lock-ups), Upendra Baxi v. State of U.P. (1983 — Agra Protective Home), People's Union for Democratic Rights v. Union of India (1982 — Asiad workers), M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (expert committees on Ganga pollution and Delhi air), and the Supreme Court's use of amicus curiae and court commissioners in the Salwa Judum, the Gujarat riots relief camps, and the pandemic migrant-worker matters. The Law Commission's 14th Report and the 1976 amendment adding Rules 10A-10C are the statutory recognition of this expansion. The rationale: the adversarial system assumes equal parties; where one party is the State or a corporation and the other is a disorganised mass of poor people, the court needs its own eyes and ears."));
c.push(H3("Minors and persons of unsound mind — Order XXXII"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: every suit by a minor is instituted in his name by a person who in the suit is called the **next friend**; a suit instituted by a minor without a next friend is taken off the file (Rule 2); Rule 2A (1976): the court may order the next friend to furnish security for costs where he is not a guardian appointed by a competent authority; **Rule 3:** where the defendant is a minor the court, on being satisfied of the minority, appoints a proper person to be **guardian for the suit (guardian ad litem)** — a person of sound mind and of full age who has no interest adverse to the minor, with his consent in writing; the order is not made without notice to the minor (if above a certain age) and to the natural guardian or the person in whose care he is; the guardian continues throughout the proceedings including appeals and execution unless removed; Rule 3A (1976): a decree against a minor is not set aside merely because the next friend or guardian had an interest adverse to the minor unless it has prejudiced the minor; Rule 4: who may act as next friend or guardian — any person of sound mind and full age whose interest is not adverse; a person appointed by a court as guardian has priority; Rule 5: a next friend or guardian cannot receive money or movable property on the minor's behalf without the court's leave, and any application is made by him (Rule 5-6); **Rule 7:** a next friend or guardian shall not, without the leave of the court expressly recorded, enter into any agreement or compromise on behalf of the minor with reference to the suit; an agreement without leave is voidable against all parties other than the minor (Rule 7(2)); leave requires an affidavit of the next friend and a certificate of the pleader that the compromise is for the minor's benefit (Rule 7(1A), 1976) — Ram Chandra Arya v. Man Singh (1968): a compromise decree without leave is void as against the minor, and the minor can avoid it in the same suit or in a separate suit; Kaushalya Devi v. Baijnath Sayal (1961). Rules 8-11: retirement, removal and death of the next friend; the suit is stayed until a new one is appointed; Rule 12: a minor plaintiff on attaining majority may elect to proceed with the suit, or to abandon it, or to apply for dismissal on the ground that it was unreasonable or improper (Rule 14); Rule 13: where a minor co-plaintiff attaining majority desires to repudiate the suit he may apply to have his name struck out; Rule 15: Rules 1-14 apply, so far as may be, to persons of unsound mind and to persons who, though not so adjudged, are found by the court on inquiry to be incapable, by reason of mental infirmity, of protecting their interest when suing or being sued (1976); Rule 16: the Order does not apply to a ruler of a former Indian State. Order XXXII-A (1976): suits relating to matters concerning the family — the court's duty to make efforts for settlement, assistance of welfare experts, and in-camera proceedings — the seed of the Family Courts Act 1984."));
c.push(H3("Indigent persons — Order XXXIII"));
c.push(P("Rule 1: any suit may be instituted by an **indigent person** — a person who is not possessed of sufficient means (other than property exempt from attachment in execution and the subject-matter of the suit) to enable him to pay the fee prescribed for the plaint, or, where no fee is prescribed, who is not entitled to property worth one thousand rupees other than the exempt property and the subject-matter (Explanation I, 1976); any property acquired after the application and before decision is taken into account (Explanation II); a person suing in a representative capacity is judged by his own means (Explanation III). The Supreme Court in Union Bank of India v. Khader International Construction (2001) held that 'person' includes a juristic person — a company may sue as an indigent person. Rule 1A (1976): inquiry into the means is first made by the chief ministerial officer of the court, whose report the court may adopt or inquire itself. Rule 2-3: the application contains the particulars of a plaint and a schedule of the applicant's property, is signed and verified, and presented by the applicant in person unless exempted. **Rule 5:** the application shall be rejected where it is not framed and presented as required, where the applicant is not an indigent person, where he has within two months before the application disposed of any property fraudulently or to be able to apply, where his allegations do not show a cause of action, where he has entered into an agreement with reference to the subject-matter under which another person has obtained an interest, where the allegations show the suit is barred by law, or where another person has entered into an agreement with him to finance the litigation (1976). Rules 6-7: notice to the opposite party and the Government pleader, examination of witnesses, and the decision — the court decides whether to allow or refuse the application after hearing evidence on the question of indigence only; Rule 8: where the application is granted it is numbered and registered as the plaint, and the suit proceeds in the ordinary manner except that the plaintiff pays no court fee (nor process fees, Rule 8); Rule 9: the court may, on the application of the defendant or the Government pleader, dispauper the plaintiff if he is guilty of vexatious or improper conduct, if his means have increased, or if he has entered into an agreement with reference to the subject-matter giving another an interest; Rule 9A (1976): the court may assign a pleader to an unrepresented indigent person; Rule 10-11: where the indigent plaintiff succeeds, the court fee is recovered from the party ordered to pay costs, and where he fails, or the suit is withdrawn or dismissed for non-appearance, the court orders him to pay the court fees; Rule 11A: the Government's first charge on the subject-matter for the court fee; Rule 15: an order refusing to allow the applicant to sue as an indigent person is a bar to a subsequent application of the like nature in respect of the same right, but the applicant may institute an ordinary suit on payment of the court fee, and the limitation is computed from the date of the original application (Rule 15A, 1976); Rule 16: costs of the application; Rule 17 (1976): a defendant who wishes to plead a set-off or counter-claim may apply to do so as an indigent person; Rule 18: the Government may make provision for free legal services to indigent persons. Order XLIV: appeals by indigent persons. The purpose is access to justice under Art 39A — 'the poor should not be turned away for want of court fees' (A.A. Haja Muniuddin v. Indian Railways, 1992 — a person who cannot pay the whole fee at once may be permitted to pay in instalments under Sec 149; the inquiry is into means, not the merits)."));
c.push(H3("Death, marriage, insolvency, withdrawal and compromise — Orders XXII and XXIII"));
c.push(P("**Order XXII:** the death of a plaintiff or defendant does not cause the suit to abate if the right to sue survives (Rule 1); where one of several plaintiffs or defendants dies and the right survives to the others, the court records this and the suit proceeds (Rule 2); where a sole plaintiff, or one of several without survivorship, dies, the legal representatives are brought on record on application within the time limited by law — ninety days under Art 120 of the Limitation Act — failing which the suit **abates** as regards the deceased (Rule 3); the same for a defendant (Rule 4); Rule 4(4) (1976): the court may exempt the plaintiff from substituting the legal representatives of a non-contesting defendant; Rule 4(5) (1976): where the plaintiff was ignorant of the death and could not make the application within time, he may apply for setting aside the abatement within a further sixty days with a condonation application, and the court shall consider the ignorance as a ground; Rule 4A (1976): where a party dies without a legal representative the court may appoint the Administrator-General or an officer to represent the estate; Rule 5: determination of the question as to who is the legal representative; Rule 6: no abatement by death after the hearing and before the judgment; Rule 7-8: marriage of a female party does not cause abatement; insolvency of a plaintiff — the assignee may continue the suit; Rule 9: where a suit abates no fresh suit lies on the same cause of action, but the legal representative may apply to set aside the abatement on proving sufficient cause for not continuing the suit within time (Art 121 — sixty days; a liberal approach is taken, Sardar Amarjit Singh Kalra v. Pramod Gupta, 2003; Perumon Bhagvathy Devaswom v. Bhargavi Amma, 2008 — 'sufficient cause' judged from the diligence of the party, the nature of the case, and the absence of mala fides); Rule 10: assignment, creation or devolution of interest during the pendency of a suit — the person on whom it devolves may, by leave, continue; Rule 10A (1976): the pleader of a deceased party must inform the court of the death; Rule 11: the Order applies to appeals; Rule 12: not to proceedings in execution. **Order XXIII:** Rule 1 (1976): at any time after the institution of a suit the plaintiff may abandon the suit or a part of the claim as against all or any of the defendants; where a suit is by a minor, leave of the court is required; the court may grant the plaintiff **permission to withdraw with liberty to institute a fresh suit** if it is satisfied that the suit must fail by reason of some formal defect, or that there are sufficient grounds for allowing him to sue afresh (Rule 1(3)); where the plaintiff abandons or withdraws without such permission he is liable for costs and is precluded from instituting any fresh suit in respect of the same subject-matter (Rule 1(4)); nothing in the rule allows one of several plaintiffs to withdraw without the consent of the others (Rule 1(5)); Rule 1A (1976): a defendant may be transposed as plaintiff where a plaintiff withdraws; Rule 2: limitation for a fresh suit is not affected by the withdrawal; **Rule 3 — compromise:** where it is proved to the satisfaction of the court that a suit has been adjusted wholly or in part by any **lawful agreement or compromise in writing and signed by the parties**, or where the defendant satisfies the plaintiff in respect of the whole or part of the subject-matter, the court shall order the agreement to be recorded and pass a decree in accordance therewith so far as it relates to the parties to the suit, whether or not the subject-matter of the agreement is the same as the subject-matter of the suit (1976); the proviso: where one party alleges that an adjustment has been arrived at and the other denies it, the court shall decide the question, and no adjournment is granted for deciding it unless the court thinks fit for reasons recorded; Explanation: an agreement that is void or voidable under the Contract Act is not 'lawful'; Rule 3A (1976): **no suit shall lie to set aside a decree on the ground that the compromise was not lawful** — the remedy is an application in the same suit (Banwari Lal v. Chando Devi, 1993; Pushpa Devi Bhagat v. Rajinder Singh, 2006 — a consent decree can be challenged only by the court that recorded it, on the ground of no valid compromise, and no appeal lies against it under Sec 96(3)); Rule 3B: no agreement or compromise in a representative suit without the leave of the court after notice to the persons interested; Rule 4: the Order does not apply to execution. A compromise decree operates as an estoppel but not as res judicata since there is no adjudication (Byram Pestonji Gariwala v. Union Bank of India, 1992 — the compromise must be in writing and signed by the parties, though a pleader with authority may sign)."));
c.push(CASE("Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India (1984) 3 SCC 161","In public interest litigation the court may appoint socio-legal commissions to gather facts; their reports are prima facie evidence which the parties may controvert by affidavit; the adversarial rules of evidence are adapted to the needs of the poor."));
c.push(CASE("Union Bank of India v. Khader International Construction (2001) 5 SCC 22","'Person' in Order XXXIII Rule 1 includes a juristic person; a company may sue as an indigent person if it lacks the means to pay the court fee."));
c.push(CASE("Ram Chandra Arya v. Man Singh AIR 1968 SC 954","A compromise on behalf of a minor without the leave of the court under Order XXXII Rule 7 is void as against the minor and may be avoided in the same suit."));
c.push(CASE("Pushpa Devi Bhagat v. Rajinder Singh (2006) 5 SCC 566","A consent decree cannot be appealed under Sec 96(3) nor set aside by a separate suit (Order XXIII Rule 3A); the only remedy is an application to the court which recorded the compromise, to be decided under the proviso to Rule 3."));
c.push(CASE("Perumon Bhagvathy Devaswom v. Bhargavi Amma (2008) 8 SCC 321","The expression 'sufficient cause' in Order XXII Rule 9 must be construed liberally; the decisive question is the diligence and bona fides of the party seeking to set aside the abatement, and the merits of the case are not examined."));
c.push(DIG("Commissioners under Order XXVI now record witnesses on video, use drone surveys and GPS coordinates for local investigations, and file scientific reports (DNA, handwriting, digital forensics) that are electronic records; the commissioner's video is part of the record under Rule 10. Socio-legal commissions in PIL rely on satellite images and government databases, which the Supreme Court has accepted (the Aravalli mining and Sterlite matters). Order XXXIII inquiries into means use the Income-tax portal, bank statements and land records; several High Courts allow indigent applications to be e-filed with an affidavit of assets. Order XXII substitution applications are flagged by the e-Courts system when a death is reported, and Order XXIII Rule 3 compromises reached in online mediation (the Mediation Act 2023 permits online mediation) are recorded by the court on the signed electronic settlement."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Commission for local investigation', 'Socio-legal commission', 'Next friend and guardian ad litem', 'Indigent person', 'Abatement', 'Withdrawal with liberty', 'Compromise decree'. Part B: 'Explain the purposes for which a court may issue a commission and the evidentiary value of a commissioner's report; discuss the role of commissions in public interest litigation' — Sec 75, Order XXVI Rules 1, 4A, 9, 10A-10C, 13, Bandhua Mukti Morcha; or 'Discuss the provisions relating to suits by or against minors'. Part C: a compromise decree passed for a minor without leave — void against the minor (Ram Chandra Arya); a plaintiff dies and his son applies for substitution after five months — abatement, Rule 9 with Art 121 and liberal construction (Perumon); a company pleads inability to pay court fee — Khader International."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 10 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 10 — The Second Look: Santosh Hazari and the Ladder of Appeals"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The right of appeal · Appeals from original decrees (Sec 96-99A, Order XLI) · Appeals from appellate decrees — the substantial question of law (Sec 100-103) · Appeals from orders (Sec 104-106, Order XLIII) · General provisions and powers of the appellate court (Sec 107-108) · Appeals to the Supreme Court (Sec 109, Order XLV) · Reference, review and revision (Sec 113-115, Order XLVI-XLVII) and Article 227"));
c.push(STORY("Madhya Pradesh, 1990s. Santosh Hazari sues Purushottam Tiwari for possession of land. The trial court dismisses the suit; the first appellate court, on the same evidence, reverses the finding and decrees the suit. Tiwari files a second appeal to the High Court, which admits it without framing any substantial question of law, hears it as if it were a first appeal, reappreciates the evidence, and restores the trial court's decree. Santosh Hazari goes to the Supreme Court, which in 2001 laid down the rules that have governed second appeals since the 1976 amendment: a second appeal lies only on a **substantial question of law**, the High Court must formulate that question at the time of admission and confine the hearing to it, the respondent may argue that the question does not arise, and a question is 'substantial' if it is of general public importance or directly and substantially affects the rights of the parties and is an open question not settled by the highest court, or calls for an alternative view. The Court also explained the duty of the first appellate court: as the final court of fact it must deal with every finding of the trial court and give its own reasons, especially when it reverses. The judgment is a map of the ladder of appeals — from the trial court's decree, up to the first appeal on facts and law, to the second appeal on law alone, and to the Supreme Court — and of the side-roads of reference, review and revision that run alongside it."));
c.push(H3("The right of appeal and appeals from original decrees — Sec 96-99A and Order XLI"));
c.push(P("An appeal is a creature of statute, a vested substantive right which accrues on the date of the institution of the suit and can be taken away only by express provision (Garikapati Veeraya v. N. Subbiah Choudhry, 1957; Colonial Sugar Refining Co. v. Irving, 1905). **Sec 96:** an appeal lies from every decree passed by a court exercising original jurisdiction to the court authorised to hear appeals from its decisions; an appeal may lie from an original decree passed ex parte (96(2)); **no appeal lies from a decree passed by the court with the consent of the parties (96(3))**; and no appeal lies, except on a question of law, from a decree in a suit of the nature cognizable by a Small Cause Court when the value does not exceed ten thousand rupees (96(4), 1999). The forum: Sec 21-22 of the Andhra Pradesh Civil Courts Act and Sec 13 of the Bengal, Agra and Assam Civil Courts Act — appeals from a decree of a Subordinate Judge or Munsif lie to the District Court where the value is below a fixed figure and to the High Court above it. Who may appeal: any party aggrieved, a legal representative, a transferee of the interest, and a person not a party who is bound or prejudiced by the decree with the leave of the appellate court (State of Punjab v. Amar Singh, 1974). **Sec 97:** where a party aggrieved by a preliminary decree does not appeal from it, he is precluded from disputing its correctness in any appeal from the final decree. **Sec 98:** decision where the appeal is heard by two or more judges — the majority prevails; where there is no majority on a point of law the appeal is referred to a third judge (Art 224 and the Letters Patent). **Sec 99:** no decree shall be reversed or substantially varied, nor any case remanded, in appeal on account of any misjoinder or non-joinder of parties or causes of action or any error, defect or irregularity in any proceedings not affecting the merits of the case or the jurisdiction of the court — but non-joinder of a necessary party is excluded from the protection (1976). **Sec 99A:** no order under Sec 47 is reversed in appeal on account of an error or irregularity unless it has prejudicially affected the decision. **Order XLI:** Rule 1: the memorandum of appeal states the grounds of objection concisely, without argument, and is accompanied by a certified copy of the decree (the judgment need not be filed in some High Courts by rule); in a money decree the appellant deposits the amount or furnishes security as the court directs (Rule 1(3), 1976); Rule 2: no ground not set forth in the memorandum is urged without leave, but the court is not confined to the grounds; Rule 3: rejection or amendment of the memorandum; Rule 3A (1976): an appeal filed after limitation is accompanied by an application for condonation, and the court shall not make an order of stay until it has decided the condonation application; **Rule 5 — stay:** an appeal does not operate as a stay of proceedings under the decree except so far as the appellate court orders, and execution is not stayed merely because an appeal has been preferred; the appellate court may order stay on sufficient cause, and so may the court which passed the decree before the expiry of the period for appeal (Rule 5(2)); no stay is granted unless the court is satisfied that substantial loss may result, that the application was made without unreasonable delay, and that security has been given for the due performance of the decree (Rule 5(3)); Rule 6-8: security in the case of an order for execution, and for the restitution of property; **Rule 9 (1999):** the memorandum of appeal is registered; Rule 11: the appellate court may dismiss the appeal without sending notice to the court below and without serving notice on the respondent, after hearing the appellant — the summary dismissal at the admission stage — and where the appellant is not present the court may dismiss; Rule 11A (1976): every appeal is to be heard under Rule 11 as expeditiously as possible and endeavour made to conclude the hearing within sixty days; Rule 12-14: day for hearing, notice to the respondent, service; Rule 16-17: right to begin, dismissal for the appellant's default (Rule 17 — the Explanation added in 1976 provides that the court shall not dismiss the appeal on merits where the appellant does not appear, only for default, so that Rule 19 remains available); Rule 18-21: dismissal for the appellant's failure to deposit costs of notice, readmission of an appeal dismissed for default, and rehearing on the respondent's application where an appeal was heard ex parte against him; **Rule 22 — cross-objection:** any respondent, though he may not have appealed, may support the decree on any ground decided against him in the court below, and may take cross-objection to the decree which he could have taken by way of appeal, within one month from the service on him of notice of the day fixed for hearing; the cross-objection is in the form of a memorandum, and, if the original appeal is withdrawn or dismissed for default, may nevertheless be heard (Rule 22(4)); Rule 23: **remand** — where the court from whose decree the appeal is preferred has disposed of the suit on a preliminary point and the decree is reversed, the appellate court may remand the case with directions on what issues to try; Rule 23A (1976): remand in other cases where a retrial is considered necessary; Rule 24: where the evidence on record is sufficient the appellate court may finally determine the suit itself; Rule 25: where the lower court has omitted to frame or try an issue essential to the decision, the appellate court may frame the issue and refer it for trial, with its findings to be returned; Rule 26: objections to the findings; Rule 26A (1976): the appellate court records the reasons for remand; **Rule 27 — additional evidence:** the parties are not entitled to produce additional evidence in the appellate court, but the court may allow it where the lower court refused to admit evidence which ought to have been admitted, or where the party establishes that notwithstanding due diligence the evidence was not within his knowledge or could not be produced at the trial (1976), or where the appellate court itself requires the document or witness to enable it to pronounce judgment or for any other substantial cause — and the court records the reason (K. Venkataramiah v. A. Seetharama Reddy, 1963 — the requirement is that of the court, not of the party; Union of India v. Ibrahim Uddin, 2012 — the application is decided at the time of hearing the appeal, and additional evidence cannot be admitted merely to fill a lacuna); Rules 28-29: the mode of taking additional evidence; **Rule 30 (1976):** the appellate court, after hearing the parties and referring to any part of the proceedings, pronounces judgment in open court, at once or on a future day with notice; **Rule 31:** the judgment of the appellate court shall be in writing and shall state the points for determination, the decision thereon, the reasons for the decision, and, where the decree appealed from is reversed or varied, the relief to which the appellant is entitled; Rule 32: what the judgment may direct; **Rule 33 — the wide power of the appellate court:** it may pass any decree and make any order which ought to have been passed or made, and may pass such further or other decree or order as the case may require, and this power may be exercised in favour of all or any of the respondents or parties, although they may not have filed any appeal or objection — a power to do complete justice, exercised sparingly, and not to reopen a matter that has become final for want of appeal against a co-defendant who did not appeal (Banarsi v. Ram Phal, 2003 — Rule 33 cannot be used to grant relief to a non-appealing party against whom the decree has become final where the cross-objection route was available, unless the decree is interdependent; Mahant Dhangir v. Madan Mohan, 1987); Rule 34: dissent recorded; Rule 35-37: the decree of the appellate court, its contents and date, and certified copies. The first appellate court is the last court of facts; it must consider all the evidence and record its own reasons on every point (Santosh Hazari; Madhukar v. Sangram, 2001; H.K.N. Swami v. Irshad Basith, 2005 — a first appeal cannot be disposed of by a cryptic judgment; B.V. Nagesh v. H.V. Sreenivasa Murthy, 2010)."));
c.push(H3("Second appeals — Sec 100-103"));
c.push(P("**Sec 100 (1976):** an appeal lies to the High Court from every decree passed in appeal by any court subordinate to the High Court, **if the High Court is satisfied that the case involves a substantial question of law** (100(1)); an appeal may lie from an appellate decree passed ex parte (100(2)); the memorandum of appeal shall precisely state the substantial question of law involved (100(3)); where the High Court is satisfied that a substantial question of law is involved, **it shall formulate that question** (100(4)); and the appeal is heard on the question so formulated, the respondent being allowed at the hearing to argue that the case does not involve such question — provided that nothing takes away the power of the court to hear the appeal on any other substantial question of law not formulated by it, if it is satisfied that the case involves such question, for reasons to be recorded (100(5)). **Sec 100A (2002):** notwithstanding anything in any Letters Patent or other law, where an appeal from an original or appellate decree or order is heard and decided by a single judge of a High Court, no further appeal lies from the judgment and decree of the single judge — the abolition of the intra-court letters patent appeal in such cases (Kamal Kumar Dutta v. Ruby General Hospital, 2006 — Sec 100A bars a letters patent appeal even from an order in a Sec 115 revision or a first appeal decided by a single judge; Subal Paul v. Malina Paul, 2003 — the letters patent appeal survives against a single judge's order in an original proceeding under a special Act where Sec 100A does not apply). **Sec 101:** no second appeal lies except on the ground mentioned in Sec 100. **Sec 102 (2002):** no second appeal lies in any suit for the recovery of money not exceeding twenty-five thousand rupees. **Sec 103 (1976):** in any second appeal the High Court may, if the evidence on the record is sufficient, determine any issue necessary for the disposal of the appeal which has not been determined by the lower appellate court or by both courts, or which has been wrongly determined by reason of a decision on a substantial question of law. What is a **substantial question of law**: a question that is of general public importance or directly and substantially affects the rights of the parties, and is either an open question not finally settled by the Supreme Court, the Privy Council or the Federal Court, or is not free from difficulty, or calls for discussion of alternative views (Sir Chunilal V. Mehta and Sons v. Century Spinning and Manufacturing Co., 1962, the leading test; Santosh Hazari v. Purushottam Tiwari, 2001; Kondiba Dagadu Kadam v. Savitribai Sopan Gujar, 1999 — a finding of fact, even if erroneous, does not become a question of law; a second appeal cannot be admitted on the ground that another view is possible; Hero Vinoth v. Seshammal, 2006 — a finding of fact is open in second appeal only if it is perverse, based on no evidence, or reached by ignoring material evidence or misreading it, or by applying a wrong legal test; Gurdev Kaur v. Kaki, 2007 — the High Court cannot re-appreciate evidence under the guise of a substantial question of law; Ishwar Dass Jain v. Sohan Lal, 2000 — the question must be framed at admission and the respondent given the opportunity to contest it; Vijay Kumar Talwar v. Commissioner of Income Tax, 2011). The Law Commission's 54th Report recommended the 1976 restriction to reduce the flood of second appeals in which High Courts had been reappreciating facts under the pre-1976 wording ('the decision being contrary to law or to some usage having the force of law'). A second appeal is not entertained on a pure question of fact even if the finding is erroneous; but where the lower appellate court has drawn a wrong inference from proved facts by applying the wrong principle of law, a question of law arises (Deity Pattabhiramaswamy v. S. Hanymayya, 1959)."));
c.push(H3("Appeals from orders — Sec 104-106 and Order XLIII"));
c.push(P("**Sec 104:** an appeal lies from the orders listed — an order under Sec 35A (compensatory costs), an order under Sec 91 or 92 refusing leave, an order under Sec 95 (compensation for arrest, attachment or injunction), an order imposing a fine or directing arrest or detention except in execution of a decree, and **any order made under rules from which an appeal is expressly allowed by rules** — and from no other orders (104(1)); no appeal lies from any order passed in appeal under this section (104(2)). **Order XLIII Rule 1** lists the appealable orders: (a) an order under Order VII Rule 10 returning a plaint (unless the procedure under Rule 10A was followed); (c) an order under Order IX Rule 9 rejecting an application to set aside the dismissal of a suit; (d) an order under Order IX Rule 13 rejecting an application to set aside an ex parte decree; (f) an order under Order XI Rule 21 (dismissal for non-compliance with discovery); (i) an order under Order XXI Rule 34 on objection to a draft document; (j) an order under Order XXI Rule 72 or 92 setting aside or refusing to set aside a sale; (ja) an order rejecting an application under Order XXI Rule 106(1) (1976); (k) an order under Order XXII Rule 9 refusing to set aside abatement; (l) an order under Order XXII Rule 10 giving or refusing leave; (n) an order under Order XXV Rule 2 rejecting an application to set aside dismissal for non-furnishing of security; (na) an order under Order XXIII Rule 3 recording or refusing to record an agreement, compromise or satisfaction — inserted by the 1976 amendment but **omitted** again by the same amendment's proviso? — the accepted position after 1976: Rule 1(m) (an order under Rule 3 of Order XXIII recording or refusing to record an agreement) was deleted, and Rule 1A (1976) provides that in an appeal against a decree passed in a suit after recording a compromise, or refusing to record one, it is open to the appellant to contest the decree on the ground that the compromise should, or should not, have been recorded; (p) an order under Order XXXIII Rule 5 or 7 rejecting an application to sue as an indigent person; (q) orders in interpleader suits under Order XXXV Rule 3, 4 or 6; **(r) an order under Order XXXVIII Rule 2, 3 or 6, or under Order XXXIX Rule 1, 2, 2A, 4 or 10** — the most used clause: appeals against the grant or refusal of temporary injunctions, attachment before judgment and receivers; (s) an order under Order XL Rule 1 or 4 (appointment or enforcement against a receiver); (t) an order of refusal under Order XLI Rule 19 to readmit an appeal or under Rule 21 to rehear; (u) **an order under Order XLI Rule 23 or 23A remanding a case** where an appeal would lie from the decree of the appellate court; (w) an order under Order XLVII Rule 4 granting an application for review. Rule 2: the procedure of Order XLI applies to appeals from orders. Non-appealable orders may be attacked in the appeal against the decree under **Sec 105:** no appeal lies from any order made by a court in the exercise of its original or appellate jurisdiction except as provided, but where a decree is appealed from, any error, defect or irregularity in any order affecting the decision of the case may be set forth as a ground of objection in the memorandum of appeal (105(1)); a party aggrieved by an order of remand from which an appeal lies who does not appeal is precluded from disputing its correctness later (105(2)). **Sec 106:** appeals from orders lie to the court to which an appeal would lie from the decree in the suit, or, where the order is made by a court in appeal, to the High Court. The 'appealable order' and 'decree' distinction is why Sec 2(2) excludes appealable orders: an order deciding a matter under Order XXI Rule 58, 98 or 100 has the force of a decree and is appealed as a decree, while an order under Order XXXIX is appealed as an order."));
c.push(H3("General provisions and appeals to the Supreme Court — Sec 107-112, Order XLV"));
c.push(P("**Sec 107:** the appellate court has power to determine a case finally, to remand a case, to frame issues and refer them for trial, and to take additional evidence or require such evidence to be taken; subject to the Code, it has the same powers and performs as nearly as may be the same duties as are conferred and imposed on courts of original jurisdiction. **Sec 108:** the provisions relating to appeals from original decrees apply, so far as may be, to appeals from appellate decrees and from orders. **Sec 109 (as substituted in 1973):** subject to the Constitution and to the rules of the Supreme Court, an appeal lies to the Supreme Court from any judgment, decree or final order in a civil proceeding of a High Court if the High Court **certifies** (i) that the case involves a substantial question of law of general importance, and (ii) that in the opinion of the High Court the said question needs to be decided by the Supreme Court — the certificate under **Art 133(1)** of the Constitution, read with Art 134A (the High Court may certify on its own motion or on an oral application immediately after the judgment); no appeal lies to the Supreme Court from the judgment of a single judge unless Parliament provides (Art 133(3)); the alternative route is special leave under Art 136. **Sec 110-111** were repealed; **Sec 112:** saving of the Supreme Court's powers under Art 136 and of the Supreme Court Rules. **Order XLV:** the application for a certificate is made to the High Court within the period of limitation (Art 132 Limitation — ninety days? — sixty days under Art 133 of the Limitation Act), the High Court hears the opposite party and, if it grants the certificate, requires security for costs and the deposit of the expenses of translating and transmitting the record (Rule 7), admits the appeal and transmits the record (Rule 8); the High Court may stay execution pending the appeal on security (Rule 13); the Supreme Court's decree is transmitted to the High Court, which sends it to the court of first instance for execution (Rule 15). The Supreme Court under Art 136 does not ordinarily interfere with concurrent findings of fact or entertain questions not raised below, but its power is plenary."));
c.push(H3("Reference, review and revision — Sec 113-115, Orders XLVI-XLVII, and Art 227"));
c.push(P("**Sec 113 — reference:** subject to the conditions prescribed, any court may state a case and refer it for the opinion of the High Court, and the High Court may make such order thereon as it thinks fit; the proviso (1976) requires reference where a court is satisfied that a case pending before it involves a question as to the validity of any Act, Ordinance or Regulation, or a provision of one, the determination of which is necessary for the disposal of the case, and the court is of opinion that it is invalid or inoperative but has not been so declared by the High Court or the Supreme Court — the court states the case with its opinion and refers. **Order XLVI:** the reference may be made in a suit or appeal in which the decree is not subject to appeal, or in execution of such a decree, where the court entertains reasonable doubt on a question of law or usage having the force of law (Rule 1); the court may pass a decree contingent on the High Court's decision (Rule 2); the High Court decides the point and the lower court disposes of the case accordingly (Rule 3); Rule 4A (1976): the reference under the proviso to Sec 113; Rule 6-7: where a court considers it has no jurisdiction it may refer, and the District Court may refer a case in which a subordinate court has acted without jurisdiction. **Sec 114 and Order XLVII — review:** any person considering himself aggrieved by a decree or order from which an appeal is allowed but from which no appeal has been preferred, or by a decree or order from which no appeal is allowed, or by a decision on a reference from a Court of Small Causes, may apply for a review of judgment to the court which passed the decree or made the order, and the court may make such order as it thinks fit; Order XLVII Rule 1 lists the grounds: **the discovery of new and important matter or evidence which, after the exercise of due diligence, was not within his knowledge or could not be produced by him at the time when the decree was passed; or some mistake or error apparent on the face of the record; or any other sufficient reason** — 'any other sufficient reason' means a reason at least analogous to the first two (Chhajju Ram v. Neki, 1922; Moran Mar Basselios Catholicos v. Most Rev. Mar Poulose Athanasius, 1954); an error apparent is one that is self-evident and does not require a long-drawn process of reasoning (Satyanarayan Laxminarayan Hegde v. Mallikarjun Bhavanappa Tirumale, 1960; Parsion Devi v. Sumitri Devi, 1997 — review is not an appeal in disguise; a rehearing on merits is not permitted; Kamlesh Verma v. Mayawati, 2013 — a summary of the grounds); the Explanation (1976): the fact that the decision on a question of law on which the judgment is based has been reversed or modified by a subsequent decision of a superior court in another case is not a ground for review; the application is made within thirty days (Art 124 Limitation); Rule 2-3: to whom the application is made and its form; Rule 4: rejection where there is no sufficient ground, or where the application is for the discovery of new matter and the applicant does not prove strict proof of due diligence; Rule 5: where the judge who passed the decree is available, he alone hears the review; Rule 7: an order rejecting a review is not appealable, but an order granting it may be objected to on limited grounds (Order XLIII Rule 1(w)); Rule 8: on the grant of review the case is reheard; Rule 9: no application to review an order made on review. **Sec 115 — revision:** the High Court may call for the record of any case which has been decided by any court subordinate to it and in which no appeal lies to the High Court, and if the subordinate court appears (a) to have exercised a jurisdiction not vested in it by law, or (b) to have failed to exercise a jurisdiction so vested, or (c) to have acted in the exercise of its jurisdiction illegally or with material irregularity, the High Court may make such order in the case as it thinks fit — **provided that (1999) the High Court shall not vary or reverse any order made, or any order deciding an issue, in the course of a suit or other proceeding, except where the order, if it had been made in favour of the party applying for revision, would have finally disposed of the suit or other proceeding**; the High Court shall not vary or reverse any decree or order against which an appeal lies either to the High Court or to any subordinate court (115(2)); and a revision does not operate as a stay of the suit except where the High Court stays it (115(3), 1999). The 1999 proviso drastically narrowed revision: interlocutory orders on amendment, injunction, evidence and the like are no longer revisable (Shiv Shakti Co-operative Housing Society v. Swaraj Developers, 2003 — the test is whether the order, if reversed, would finally dispose of the suit; an order rejecting an application for rejection of the plaint is not revisable); Sec 115 is confined to jurisdictional errors — 'acted illegally or with material irregularity' refers to the manner of exercising jurisdiction, not to the correctness of the decision (Baldevdas Shivlal v. Filmistan Distributors, 1970; D.L.F. Housing and Construction Co. v. Sarup Singh, 1971; Major S.S. Khanna v. Brig. F.J. Dillon, 1964 — a decision on an issue that finally decides part of the case may be revised). The gap left by the 1999 amendment is filled by **Art 227** of the Constitution, the High Court's power of superintendence over all courts and tribunals, which the Supreme Court in Surya Dev Rai v. Ram Chander Rai (2003) held is available against interlocutory orders of civil courts, though only for jurisdictional errors, grave injustice or perversity (Radhey Shyam v. Chhabi Nath, 2015 — a Constitution Bench held that Art 226 writ of certiorari does not lie against the orders of civil courts, but Art 227 does); the Andhra Pradesh and Telangana High Courts entertain 'civil revision petitions' under Art 227 for the orders that Sec 115 no longer reaches. The State amendments (Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh) have their own Sec 115 giving revisional power to District Judges as well."));
c.push(CASE("Santosh Hazari v. Purushottam Tiwari (2001) 3 SCC 179","A second appeal lies only on a substantial question of law which the High Court must formulate at admission and confine the hearing to; a first appellate court, as the final court of fact, must record its own reasons on every point, especially when reversing the trial court."));
c.push(CASE("Sir Chunilal V. Mehta and Sons v. Century Spinning and Manufacturing Co. AIR 1962 SC 1314","The test of a substantial question of law: general public importance or direct and substantial effect on the rights of the parties, and an open question not settled by the highest court or not free from difficulty."));
c.push(CASE("Garikapati Veeraya v. N. Subbiah Choudhry AIR 1957 SC 540","The right of appeal is a vested substantive right that accrues on the date of institution of the suit and is governed by the law then in force; it can be taken away only by express words or necessary intendment."));
c.push(CASE("Shiv Shakti Co-operative Housing Society v. Swaraj Developers (2003) 6 SCC 659","After the 1999 proviso to Sec 115, an interlocutory order is revisable only if, had it been made in favour of the applicant, it would have finally disposed of the suit; other interlocutory orders are outside revision."));
c.push(CASE("Surya Dev Rai v. Ram Chander Rai (2003) 6 SCC 675 and Radhey Shyam v. Chhabi Nath (2015) 5 SCC 423","The High Court's supervisory jurisdiction under Art 227 is available against interlocutory orders of civil courts for jurisdictional errors and grave injustice; certiorari under Art 226 does not lie against civil court orders (Radhey Shyam overruling Surya Dev Rai on that point)."));
c.push(DIG("Appeals are e-filed with the digitally certified copy of the decree, and the 'substantial question of law' must be stated in a separate paragraph of the e-form in most High Courts; the Supreme Court's e-filing requires the Art 133 certificate or the SLP paper-book in digital form. Review petitions are decided in chambers by circulation, and the Supreme Court has held that the 'error apparent' test applies to judgments uploaded with clerical mistakes corrected under Sec 152. Under the Commercial Courts Act appeals from Commercial Courts lie to a Commercial Appellate Division within sixty days, and no revision lies at all against interlocutory orders in commercial suits (Sec 8), pushing parties to Art 227."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Substantial question of law', 'Cross-objection', 'Remand', 'Additional evidence in appeal', 'Review', 'Revision', 'Reference', 'Sec 100A', 'Consent decree not appealable'. Part B: 'Discuss the scope of a second appeal under Sec 100 after the 1976 amendment' — text, the Chunilal Mehta test, Santosh Hazari, Kondiba, Hero Vinoth, Sec 101-103; or 'Explain the grounds of review and distinguish review from appeal and revision'; or 'What are the powers of a first appellate court? (Sec 107, Order XLI Rules 23-33)'. Part C: a High Court admits a second appeal to reappreciate evidence — not permissible (Gurdev Kaur); an order refusing amendment challenged in revision after 1999 — not revisable (Shiv Shakti), go under Art 227 or wait for the appeal against the decree (Sec 105); a respondent who did not appeal seeks a larger relief — Rule 33 versus the cross-objection route (Banarsi)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 11 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 11 — Time Bars the Door: Katiji and the Limitation Act"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The concept and rationale of limitation · The scheme of the Limitation Act 1963 (Sec 2-32, excluding the Schedule) · The bar (Sec 3) · Extension for sufficient cause (Sec 5) · Legal disability (Sec 6-9) · Computation and exclusion of time (Sec 12-17) · Acknowledgment and part payment (Sec 18-21) · Continuing wrongs (Sec 22-23) · Acquisition of easements and extinguishment of rights (Sec 25-27)"));
c.push(STORY("Jammu, 1987. The Collector of Land Acquisition, Anantnag, has acquired land belonging to Mst Katiji and others, and the reference court has enhanced the compensation. The State files its appeal to the High Court four days late. The High Court dismisses the appeal as time-barred and refuses to condone the delay. The Supreme Court, through Justice Thakkar, reversed in a short judgment that has been cited in tens of thousands of cases since: the expression 'sufficient cause' in Section 5 of the Limitation Act is 'adequately elastic to enable the courts to apply the law in a meaningful manner which subserves the ends of justice'. The Court set out six principles: ordinarily a litigant does not stand to benefit by lodging an appeal late; refusing to condone delay can result in a meritorious matter being thrown out at the threshold and the cause of justice defeated, while condoning it means only that the case will be decided on merits; 'every day's delay must be explained' does not mean a pedantic approach; when substantial justice and technical considerations are pitted against each other, substantial justice deserves to be preferred; there is no presumption that delay is deliberate or on account of culpable negligence; and the judiciary is respected not for legalising injustice on technical grounds but for removing injustice. The Court added that the State, as an impersonal machinery working through officers, deserves a certain latitude. Four days' delay was condoned. But the same Court has also reminded litigants that limitation is a rule of substance, not a technicality: the law of limitation may be harsh, but it must be applied (Basawaraj v. Land Acquisition Officer, 2013), because 'interest reipublicae ut sit finis litium' — it is in the public interest that litigation should come to an end — and because a person who sleeps over his rights cannot expect the court to wake them."));
c.push(H3("Concept and rationale"));
c.push(P("Limitation prescribes the time within which a person may enforce a right in a court; after that time the remedy is barred though the right (generally) survives. The rationale — Halsbury's three reasons: long dormant claims have more of cruelty than justice in them; a defendant might have lost the evidence to disprove a stale claim; and persons with good causes of action should pursue them with reasonable diligence. Maxims: interest reipublicae ut sit finis litium; vigilantibus non dormientibus jura subveniunt — the law assists the vigilant, not those who sleep. Limitation is a law of procedure (lex fori — the law of the forum), not of substance, so it applies to all proceedings in Indian courts whatever the law governing the right, and it is retrospective in the sense that it applies to causes of action that arose before it came into force, subject to Sec 30-31; it bars the remedy, not the right — except Sec 27, which extinguishes the right to property after the limitation for a suit for possession expires (Rajender Singh v. Santa Singh, 1973 — the object is to prevent disturbance of what has been acquired by long enjoyment and to quiet titles; Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha, 1992 — a time-barred debt is not extinguished, so a creditor may adjust a deposit against it). History: the Regulations of 1793, the Act XIV of 1859, the Acts of 1871, 1877 and 1908, and the **Limitation Act, 1963** (Act 36 of 1963, in force 1 January 1964), enacted on the Law Commission's 3rd Report, which reduced the number of Articles from 183 to 137, made the Act applicable to the whole of India, and introduced Sec 5 for all appeals and applications. Structure: Part I preliminary (Sec 1-2); Part II limitation of suits, appeals and applications (Sec 3-11); Part III computation of the period (Sec 12-24); Part IV acquisition of ownership by possession (Sec 25-27); Part V miscellaneous (Sec 28-32); the Schedule with three Divisions — suits (Art 1-113), appeals (Art 114-117), applications (Art 118-137)."));
c.push(H3("The bar — Sec 3 and 4"));
c.push(P("**Sec 3(1):** subject to Sec 4-24, every suit instituted, appeal preferred and application made after the prescribed period shall be dismissed, **although limitation has not been set up as a defence** — the court must take the point suo motu; the parties cannot waive it by agreement or consent. Sec 3(2): when a suit is deemed instituted — on presentation of the plaint, on the application to sue as an indigent person, or, for a set-off or counter-claim, on the date it is pleaded; an application by notice of motion is made when presented. The plaintiff must show that his suit is within time (Order VII Rule 6 — the plaint states the ground of exemption). **Sec 4:** where the prescribed period expires on a day when the court is closed, the suit, appeal or application may be instituted on the day the court reopens (the Explanation: a court is deemed closed on any day it is closed during any part of its normal working hours) — Sec 4 applies to the 'prescribed period', not to a period extended under Sec 5, and not to the period of limitation prescribed by a special Act unless Sec 29(2) applies. **Sec 29:** the savings — Sec 29(2): where a special or local law prescribes a period different from the Schedule, Sec 3 applies as if the period were prescribed by the Schedule, and Sec 4-24 apply only in so far as they are not expressly excluded by the special law (Hukumdev Narain Yadav v. Lalit Narain Mishra, 1974 — the Representation of the People Act excludes Sec 5 by necessary implication; Union of India v. Popular Construction Co., 2001 — Sec 34(3) of the Arbitration Act excludes Sec 5; Consolidated Engineering Enterprises v. Principal Secretary, Irrigation Department, 2008 — Sec 14 applies to arbitration applications because it is not expressly excluded); Sec 29(3): the Act does not apply to suits under the marriage laws except as expressly provided."));
c.push(H3("Extension — Sec 5"));
c.push(P("Any **appeal or any application, other than an application under any of the provisions of Order XXI of the Code**, may be admitted after the prescribed period if the appellant or the applicant satisfies the court that he had **sufficient cause** for not preferring the appeal or making the application within such period; the Explanation: the fact that the appellant or applicant was misled by any order, practice or judgment of the High Court in ascertaining or computing the period may be sufficient cause. Sec 5 does **not** apply to suits (the plaintiff's remedy is the Schedule's own extensions and Sec 6-23) nor to execution applications under Order XXI, and it applies to special-law proceedings only through Sec 29(2). 'Sufficient cause' is a fact-based, liberal but not indulgent test: Collector, Land Acquisition v. Mst Katiji (1987) (the six principles); N. Balakrishnan v. M. Krishnamurthy (1998) — the length of delay is no matter; acceptability of the explanation is the only criterion; but the discretion must be exercised judiciously, and once the explanation is found not to be bona fide, delay cannot be condoned; Ramlal v. Rewa Coalfields (1962) — the applicant must explain the delay up to the date of filing, and after sufficient cause is shown the court still has a discretion, which considers the diligence and bona fides; State of West Bengal v. Administrator, Howrah Municipality (1972) — the words should receive a liberal construction to advance substantial justice where no negligence or inaction or want of bona fides is imputable; Basawaraj v. Special Land Acquisition Officer (2013) — 'sufficient cause' means a cause beyond the control of the party, and the court cannot condone delay where the party has been negligent; Esha Bhattacharjee v. Managing Committee of Raghunathpur Nafar Academy (2013) — a summary of the principles: liberal, pragmatic, justice-oriented, but not a licence for negligence; the conduct of the party is relevant; the concept of 'sufficient cause' is not to be stretched to the point of subverting the statute; Postmaster General v. Living Media India (2012) — the Government cannot claim latitude for its own red tape; Lanka Venkateswarlu v. State of A.P. (2011) — the Supreme Court's condonation of 3703 days was set aside as 'perverse'; Ketan V. Parekh (2011) — no condonation where the delay is deliberate; Mst Katiji latitude for the State was qualified in Living Media. The 2020-2022 orders of the Supreme Court in In re: Cognizance for Extension of Limitation excluded the period from 15 March 2020 to 28 February 2022 from limitation for all proceedings in view of the pandemic — an exercise of Art 142, not of Sec 5. Mistake of counsel may be sufficient cause if bona fide (Rafiq v. Munshilal, 1981; but not where the litigant himself was negligent — Salil Dutta v. T.M. and M.C. Pvt. Ltd., 1993)."));
c.push(H3("Legal disability — Sec 6-9"));
c.push(P("**Sec 6:** where a person entitled to institute a suit or make an application for the execution of a decree is, at the time from which the prescribed period is to be reckoned, a minor, insane or an idiot, he may institute the suit or make the application within the same period after the disability has ceased as would otherwise have been allowed from the time specified in the Schedule; where the person is affected by two disabilities, or one disability is followed by another before the first ceases, the period runs from the cessation of both; where the disability continues till death, his legal representative may sue within the same period after the death; and where the representative is himself under disability, the same rules apply to him; Sec 6(5): where a person under disability dies after the disability ceases but within the period allowed, his legal representative may sue within the same period after the death as would otherwise have been available to that person. Sec 6 applies only to **suits and applications for execution**, not to appeals or other applications, and only to the person entitled to sue at the start of the period — a disability arising later does not stop time (Sec 9). **Sec 7:** where one of several persons jointly entitled to institute a suit or make an application is under disability, and a discharge can be given without the concurrence of such person, time runs against them all; but where no such discharge can be given, time does not run against any of them until one of them becomes capable of giving the discharge without the concurrence of the others or until the disability has ceased (Explanation I — the manager of a Hindu undivided family governed by Mitakshara law is deemed capable of giving a discharge without the concurrence of the other members only if he is in management of the joint family property; Explanation II — a discharge by a Muslim guardian). **Sec 8:** nothing in Sec 6 or 7 applies to suits to enforce rights of pre-emption, or shall be deemed to extend, for more than **three years from the cessation of the disability or the death** of the person affected, the period of limitation for any suit or application — the ceiling. **Sec 9:** where once time has begun to run, no subsequent disability or inability to institute a suit or make an application stops it — the rule of continuous running; the proviso: where letters of administration to the estate of a creditor have been granted to his debtor, the running of the period for a suit to recover the debt is suspended while the administration continues. Illustration: a minor's right accrues at twelve; the period is three years; he attains majority at eighteen; he may sue till twenty-one (Sec 6) — and Sec 8 caps the extension at three years after cessation, so where the ordinary period is twelve years (a suit for possession) and the disability ceases after it has run out, the suit must be brought within three years of majority."));
c.push(H3("Computation — Sec 12-17"));
c.push(P("**Sec 12:** in computing the period for any suit, appeal or application, the day from which the period is to be reckoned is excluded (12(1)); in computing the period for an appeal or an application for leave to appeal, or for revision or review of a judgment, the day on which the judgment complained of was pronounced and **the time requisite for obtaining a copy of the decree, sentence or order appealed from or sought to be revised or reviewed** are excluded (12(2)); where a decree or order is appealed from, the time requisite for obtaining a copy of the judgment is also excluded (12(3)); the time requisite for obtaining a copy of the award is excluded in an application to set aside an award (12(4)); the Explanation (1963): in computing the time requisite for obtaining a copy, any time taken by the court to prepare the decree or order before an application for a copy is made is not excluded — Udayan Chinubhai v. R.C. Bali (1977) and State of U.P. v. Maharaja Narain (1968): 'time requisite' is the time properly required, not the time actually taken by a dilatory applicant; the period between the application for a copy and its readiness is excluded. **Sec 13:** where a person applies to sue or appeal as an indigent person, the time during which the application is pending is excluded if it is rejected and the fee is paid within the time allowed. **Sec 14 — exclusion of time of proceeding bona fide in a court without jurisdiction:** in computing the period for any suit, the time during which the plaintiff has been prosecuting with due diligence another civil proceeding, whether in a court of first instance or of appeal or revision, against the defendant is excluded, where the proceeding relates to the same matter in issue and is prosecuted in good faith in a court which, from defect of jurisdiction or other cause of a like nature, is unable to entertain it (14(1)); the same for applications (14(2)); and (14(3)) for a fresh suit instituted on permission granted under Order XXIII Rule 1 on the ground that the first suit must fail by reason of a defect in the jurisdiction of the court or other cause of a like nature; the Explanations: the day of institution and the day of the ending of the proceeding are both counted; a plaintiff or applicant resisting an appeal is deemed to be prosecuting a proceeding; misjoinder of parties or causes of action is deemed a cause of a like nature with defect of jurisdiction. Conditions (Consolidated Engineering Enterprises, 2008): both proceedings are civil proceedings prosecuted by the same party; the earlier proceeding was prosecuted with due diligence and in good faith (Sec 2(h): 'good faith' means with due care and attention); the failure was due to defect of jurisdiction or other cause of a like nature; the earlier and later proceedings relate to the same matter in issue; and the earlier proceeding was in a court. Sec 14 applies to arbitration applications under Sec 34 of the 1996 Act (Consolidated Engineering) and, by analogy, is extended by the courts to other proceedings; it does not apply to appeals but the principle may be invoked under Sec 5 for appeals. The return of a plaint under Order VII Rule 10 followed by representation is governed by Sec 14 (the fresh suit is instituted on the date of representation, and the time in the wrong court is excluded). **Sec 15:** exclusion of the time during which proceedings are stayed by injunction or order (15(1)); the time for a notice required by law (Sec 80 CPC — two months excluded, 15(2)) or for obtaining the previous consent or sanction of the Government (15(2)); the period during which the defendant was absent from India (15(5)); the time during which the previous suit was pending in insolvency proceedings against the defendant (15(3)-(4)). **Sec 16:** where a person who would have been entitled to sue dies before the right accrues, or the right accrues on his death, the period is computed from the time there is a legal representative capable of suing; the same for a defendant who dies before the right accrues against him — but not for suits to enforce rights of pre-emption or for possession of immovable property or a hereditary office (16(3)). **Sec 17 — fraud or mistake:** where, in the case of any suit or application for which a period of limitation is prescribed, (a) the suit or application is based upon the fraud of the defendant or respondent or his agent, or (b) the knowledge of the right or title on which a suit or application is founded is concealed by the fraud of any such person, or (c) the suit or application is for relief from the consequences of a mistake, or (d) where any document necessary to establish the right of the plaintiff or applicant has been fraudulently concealed from him — the period shall not begin to run until the plaintiff or applicant has discovered the fraud or the mistake or could, with reasonable diligence, have discovered it, or in the case of a concealed document until he first had the means of producing it or compelling its production — with the proviso that nothing enables a suit to be instituted to recover, or enforce a charge against, or set aside a transaction affecting, property which has been purchased for valuable consideration by a person not a party to the fraud and who did not know or have reason to believe of the fraud, or, in the case of mistake, property purchased for value after the transaction by a person who did not know of the mistake; Sec 17(2): where a judgment-debtor has by fraud or force prevented the execution of a decree within the period, the court may on application made after the expiry of the period extend it. Sec 17 postpones the start of limitation; it does not extend it (Pallav Sheth v. Custodian, 2001 — the principle of Sec 17 applies to contempt proceedings; Ramaiah v. N. Narayana Reddy, 2004)."));
c.push(H3("Acknowledgment, part payment and other provisions — Sec 18-24"));
c.push(P("**Sec 18 — effect of acknowledgment in writing:** where, **before the expiration of the prescribed period** for a suit or application in respect of any property or right, an **acknowledgment of liability** in respect of such property or right has been made **in writing signed by the party** against whom such property or right is claimed, or by any person through whom he derives his title or liability, a fresh period of limitation shall be computed from the time when the acknowledgment was so signed; Sec 18(2): where the writing is undated, oral evidence may be given of the time it was signed, but oral evidence of its contents is not received; the Explanation: an acknowledgment may be sufficient though it omits to specify the exact nature of the property or right, or avers that the time for payment has not yet come, or is accompanied by a refusal to pay, or is coupled with a claim to set-off, or is addressed to a person other than the person entitled; 'signed' means signed personally or by an agent duly authorised; an application for the execution of a decree is not an acknowledgment. Essentials: an admission of a subsisting liability (a mere reference to a past liability is not enough), in writing, signed, made before the period expires, by the party or his agent, and the words must indicate a jural relationship — the Supreme Court in Shapoor Freedom Mazda v. Durga Prasad Chamaria (1961) and Khan Bahadur Shapoor Fredoom Mazda laid down that the statement must relate to a present subsisting liability and indicate the existence of a jural relationship, and the words need not be an express promise; a balance-sheet entry of a debt is an acknowledgment (Mahabir Cold Storage v. CIT, 1991; Asset Reconstruction Co. v. Bishal Jaiswal, 2021 — under the IBC, entries in balance sheets are acknowledgments for Sec 18, subject to explanatory notes); an acknowledgment made after the expiry of the period does not revive the debt — but a promise to pay a time-barred debt in writing is enforceable under Sec 25(3) of the Contract Act. **Sec 19 — effect of payment:** where payment on account of a debt or of interest on a legacy is made before the expiration of the prescribed period by the person liable or his agent, a fresh period is computed from the time the payment was made — provided that, except in the case of payment of interest made before 1 January 1928, an acknowledgment of the payment appears in the handwriting of, or in a writing signed by, the person making the payment; the Explanation: where mortgaged land is in the possession of the mortgagee, the receipt of rent or produce is a payment; 'debt' does not include money payable under a decree or order. A payment by cheque is a payment on the date the cheque is handed over if it is honoured, and the signed cheque is the acknowledgment of the payment (Jiwanlal Achariya v. Rameshwarlal Agarwalla, 1967). **Sec 20:** the effect of acknowledgment or payment by another person — the agent duly authorised includes a lawful guardian, a committee or manager of a person under disability, and an agent authorised by them; nothing in Sec 18 or 19 renders one of several joint contractors, partners, executors or mortgagees chargeable by reason only of a written acknowledgment or payment made by another of them; and Sec 20(3): a receipt of rent or produce by the mortgagee. **Sec 21:** where after the institution of a suit a new plaintiff or defendant is substituted or added, the suit is deemed to have been instituted as regards him when he was so made a party, unless the court is satisfied that the omission was due to a mistake made in good faith, in which case it may direct that the suit be deemed instituted on an earlier date; the substitution of a legal representative, or the transposition of parties, is not a substitution for this purpose (21(2)). **Sec 22 — continuing breaches and torts:** in the case of a continuing breach of contract or a continuing tort, a fresh period of limitation begins to run at every moment of the time during which the breach or the tort continues (a continuing wrong is one that creates a continuing source of injury, e.g. an obstruction to a right of way; not the continuing effect of a completed wrong — Balakrishna Savalram Pujari Waghmare v. Shree Dhyaneshwar Maharaj Sansthan, 1959; M. Siddiq (Ayodhya), 2019). **Sec 23:** in a suit for compensation for an act which does not give rise to a cause of action unless some specific injury actually results, the period is computed from the time the injury results. **Sec 24:** all instruments are deemed made with reference to the Gregorian calendar."));
c.push(H3("Acquisition of easements and extinguishment of the right — Sec 25-27"));
c.push(P("**Sec 25 — acquisition of easements by prescription:** where the access and use of light or air to and for any building have been peaceably enjoyed therewith as an easement, and as of right, without interruption, and for twenty years, and where any way or watercourse, or the use of any water, or any other easement (whether affirmative or negative) has been peaceably and openly enjoyed by any person claiming title thereto as an easement and as of right without interruption and for twenty years, the right to such access and use of light or air, way, watercourse, use of water, or other easement becomes absolute and indefeasible; the period is thirty years where the property belongs to the Government; each of the periods of twenty and thirty years is taken as ending within two years next before the institution of the suit in which the claim is contested (25(2)); Sec 25(3): nothing is an interruption unless there is an actual discontinuance of possession or enjoyment by reason of an obstruction by the act of some person other than the claimant, and unless the obstruction is submitted to or acquiesced in for one year after the claimant has notice of it and of the person making it. The Explanation and Sec 26: the exclusion of the time during which the servient tenement was held under a term of life or of years exceeding three years, if the claim is resisted within three years after the term ends. Sec 25 of the Limitation Act is superseded in States where the Indian Easements Act 1882 applies (Sec 15 of that Act is identical), including Andhra Pradesh. **Sec 27 — extinguishment of right to property:** at the determination of the period hereby limited to any person for instituting a suit for possession of any property, his right to such property shall be extinguished — the one provision of the Act that destroys the right and not merely the remedy, and the statutory foundation of **adverse possession**: a person in possession of another's land, openly, continuously, hostile to the true owner's title, for twelve years (Art 65 — from when the possession becomes adverse; Art 64 — from dispossession, for a suit based on previous possession; Art 112 — thirty years for the Government), acquires title as the owner's right is extinguished; the possession must be nec vi, nec clam, nec precario — not by force, not by stealth, not by permission (Karnataka Board of Wakf v. Government of India, 2004 — the classical requirements: the possessor must plead and prove the date and nature of his possession, its openness and the knowledge of the true owner, and its continuity; Hemaji Waghaji Jat v. Bhikhabhai Khengarbhai Harijan, 2008 — the Supreme Court doubted the justice of the doctrine and recommended reconsideration; Ravinder Kaur Grewal v. Manjit Kaur, 2019 — a three-judge bench held that a person who has perfected title by adverse possession may not only defend but also sue on that title — adverse possession is a sword as well as a shield; State of Haryana v. Mukesh Kumar, 2011 — the State cannot claim adverse possession against citizens' land except in rare cases, and the Court called for the law to be amended; Chatti Konati Rao v. Palle Venkata Subba Rao, 2010 — permissive possession never becomes adverse until an open assertion of hostile title). A co-owner's possession is not adverse to other co-owners without ouster (P. Lakshmi Reddy v. L. Lakshmi Reddy, 1957)."));
c.push(H3("Miscellaneous — Sec 28-32"));
c.push(P("Sec 28: amendment of the Indian Easements Act. **Sec 29:** savings — 29(1): the Act does not affect Sec 25 of the Contract Act; 29(2): special and local laws (above); 29(3): marriage laws; 29(4): the Act does not apply to suits under the Divorce Act? — the position is that the Act is excluded where a special law provides its own scheme (e.g. the Representation of the People Act, the Motor Vehicles Act after 1994 which removed the period of limitation for claims). Sec 30: transitional provision for suits, appeals and applications for which the period under the 1963 Act is shorter than under the 1908 Act — seven years' grace (now spent, except for Jammu and Kashmir after 2019). Sec 31: the Act does not revive a remedy barred under the 1908 Act nor affect suits instituted before it came into force. Sec 32: repeal. The **Schedule** (excluded from the syllabus but the principal Articles should be known): Art 54 — specific performance, three years from the date fixed for performance or, if none, from notice of refusal; Art 58 — declaration, three years from when the right to sue first accrues; Art 59 — cancellation of an instrument, three years from knowledge; Art 64-65 — possession, twelve years; Art 113 — the residuary, three years from when the right to sue accrues; Art 116-117 — appeals to the High Court ninety days, to other courts thirty days; Art 120-121 — substitution of legal representatives ninety days, setting aside abatement sixty days; Art 123 — setting aside an ex parte decree thirty days; Art 124 — review thirty days; Art 127 — setting aside a court sale sixty days; Art 136 — execution of a decree twelve years; Art 137 — the residuary for applications, three years."));
c.push(CASE("Collector, Land Acquisition, Anantnag v. Mst. Katiji (1987) 2 SCC 107","'Sufficient cause' under Sec 5 is elastic and justice-oriented; six principles favouring condonation where substantial justice requires it; the State deserves some latitude as an impersonal litigant."));
c.push(CASE("Basawaraj v. Special Land Acquisition Officer (2013) 14 SCC 81","Sufficient cause means a cause beyond the control of the party; the law of limitation may be harsh but must be applied; negligence and want of bona fides defeat condonation; Katiji is not a licence."));
c.push(CASE("Consolidated Engineering Enterprises v. Principal Secretary, Irrigation Department (2008) 7 SCC 169","The five conditions for Sec 14; the section applies to an application under Sec 34 of the Arbitration Act because it is not expressly excluded by Sec 29(2); the policy of Sec 14 is to advance the cause of justice."));
c.push(CASE("Sarah Mathew v. Institute of Cardio Vascular Diseases (2014) 2 SCC 62","(Criminal limitation, but the principle of Sec 3(2) is parallel) — the date of filing, not the date the court acts, is the relevant date."));
c.push(CASE("Ravinder Kaur Grewal v. Manjit Kaur (2019) 8 SCC 729","A person who has perfected title by adverse possession under Sec 27 read with Art 65 can file a suit to protect or recover possession; adverse possession is both a shield and a sword."));
c.push(DIG("Limitation now runs on electronic timestamps: e-filing portals record the date and time of presentation for Sec 3(2), and the Supreme Court's pandemic orders excluded 15 March 2020 to 28 February 2022 from every limitation period, a fact the e-Courts limitation calculator applies automatically. The time requisite for obtaining a copy under Sec 12 is computed from the online copy-application and the certified digital copy's issue date. Balance-sheet acknowledgments under Sec 18 are filed on the MCA portal in XBRL form and were held to be acknowledgments in Bishal Jaiswal (2021). WhatsApp messages admitting a debt satisfy the 'writing signed' requirement of Sec 18 only if they are proved as electronic records with an electronic signature or authenticated under the IT Act — an open question that trial courts are beginning to face."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Sufficient cause', 'Legal disability', 'Acknowledgment', 'Sec 14', 'Continuing wrong', 'Adverse possession', 'Sec 27', 'Sec 29(2)'. Part B: 'Explain the doctrine of sufficient cause under Sec 5 with reference to leading cases' — text, exclusions (suits, Order XXI), Katiji principles, Balakrishnan, Basawaraj, Esha Bhattacharjee, Living Media, the State's position; or 'Discuss the effect of acknowledgment and part payment on the period of limitation' — Sec 18 essentials with Shapoor Freedom Mazda and the Explanation, Sec 19 with the handwriting requirement, Sec 20, and the difference from Sec 25(3) Contract Act; or 'What is legal disability and how does it affect limitation?' — Sec 6-9 with illustrations. Part C: a minor of ten when the cause of action for possession arose sues at twenty-two — Sec 6 gives twelve years from majority but Sec 8 caps at three years after majority, so barred; a suit filed in the wrong court for two years, returned, and re-presented — Sec 14 excludes the time; a debtor writes 'I will pay when I can' — an acknowledgment (Explanation to Sec 18)."));
c.push(LN());

// ---------- EP 12 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 12 — Settling Outside the Court: Afcons and the Reform of Civil Procedure"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The 1976, 1999 and 2002 amendments · Section 89 and alternative dispute resolution · The Salem Advocate cases · The Commercial Courts Act 2015 and its Orders · Summary suits (Order XXXVII) · The Mediation Act 2023 and pre-institution mediation · Case management, e-filing and the future of civil procedure"));
c.push(STORY("Kochi, 2010. Cherian Varkey Construction Company sues Afcons Infrastructure and the Cochin Port Trust over a sub-contract for a bridge; the trial court, using its new power under Section 89, refers the dispute to arbitration without the consent of Afcons, which objects that it never agreed to arbitrate. The Supreme Court used the case to explain a section that had puzzled trial courts since 2002. Section 89, it said, is badly drafted — it mixes up 'mediation' and 'judicial settlement' and asks the court to 'formulate the terms of settlement' before referring, which is impossible — and the Court read it as Parliament must have meant: the court must consider, after the pleadings and the Order X examination, whether the case is suitable for one of five processes — arbitration, conciliation, judicial settlement, Lok Adalat or mediation; arbitration and conciliation require the consent of all parties because they take the case out of the court permanently; the other three do not; the court need only make a brief reference to the nature of the dispute, not draft terms; and certain categories — representative suits, election disputes, cases of fraud or forgery, suits for declaration of title against the Government, and grave criminal-flavoured matters — are unsuitable, while contract, family, partition, money and consumer disputes are suitable. The reference to arbitration was set aside. Afcons is the story of how civil procedure changed direction at the turn of the century: from trial as the only road to judgment, to settlement as the first road and trial as the last; from unlimited adjournments to time limits; from paper to e-filing; and, after 2015, to a separate track for commercial disputes. It closes the syllabus by asking what the Code will look like in the next decade."));
c.push(H3("The amending Acts"));
c.push(P("**The 1976 amendment (Act 104 of 1976)** followed the Law Commission's 27th, 40th and 54th Reports: Sec 25 transfer by the Supreme Court; Sec 21A no suit to set aside a decree on a place-of-suing objection; Explanations VII and VIII to Sec 11; Sec 80(2)-(3) urgent suits and defects in notice; Sec 96(3) — the earlier text — and Sec 100 restricted to substantial questions of law; Sec 115 provisos; Order I Rule 8 representative suits; Order VII Rule 10A; Order VIII Rule 6A counter-claim; Order IX Rule 13 provisos; Order XVIII Rule 3A; Order XXI Rules 41 (affidavit of assets), 46A-46I garnishee, 58 claims, 97-103 obstruction; Order XXII Rule 4(4)-(5); Order XXIII Rule 3 proviso and 3A; Order XXVI Rules 10A-10C; Order XXVII Rules 5A-5B; Order XXXII Rule 3A, 7(1A); Order XXXII-A family matters; Order XXXIII Rule 1 Explanations; Order XXXIX Rules 2A, 3 proviso, 3A; Order XLI Rules 1(3), 3A, 11A, 22, 23A, 26A, 27(1)(aa), 30, 33. **The 1999 amendment (Act 46 of 1999) and the 2002 amendment (Act 22 of 2002)**, both in force from 1 July 2002, followed the Malimath Committee on Arrears (1990), the Law Commission's 129th Report (urban litigation and mediation) and the Justice V.S. Malimath Committee: Sec 27 summons within thirty days; Sec 32 fine limit; Sec 35A compensatory costs raised; Sec 39(3)-(4) execution outside local limits; Sec 58 and 60 figures; Sec 64(2); Sec 89 settlement of disputes outside the court; Sec 95 limit; Sec 96(4); Sec 100A; Sec 102; Sec 115 proviso; Order V Rule 9 modes of service and Rule 9A; Order VI Rule 15(4) affidavit, Rule 17 proviso and Rule 18; Order VII Rule 9, 11(e)-(f), 14; Order VIII Rule 1 thirty/ninety days, Rule 1A, 10; Order IX Rule 5; Order X Rules 1A-1C; Order XI Rule 15; Order XIII Rule 1; Order XIV Rule 5; Order XVI Rule 1(1); Order XVII Rule 1 three adjournments and the provisos; Order XVIII Rules 2(3A), 4 affidavits, 5, 19; Order XX Rules 1, 6B; Order XXI Rules 32, 92(2) (ninety days? — sixty days under the Limitation Act Art 127), 106; Order XLI Rule 9 and the deletion of Rules 15-16 and 27's? — the 1999 Act's more drastic proposals (deleting Order XVIII Rule 17A, Order XLI Rule 33? no) were softened by the 2002 Act after protests by the Bar, which is what Salem Advocate (I) and (II) validated. **The Commercial Courts, Commercial Division and Commercial Appellate Division of High Courts Act 2015** (amended in 2018): for 'commercial disputes' of a 'specified value' (three lakh rupees after 2018) the Code applies with amendments in the Schedule — Order VI Rule 15A statement of truth; Order VII Rule 14 and Order VIII Rule 1A replaced by a duty of disclosure of all documents; Order VIII Rule 1 written statement within thirty days, extendable to one hundred and twenty days, after which the right is forfeited; Order XI rewritten (disclosure, discovery, inspection, admission and production of documents, electronic records); **Order XIII-A summary judgment** — a court may decide a claim without recording oral evidence where the plaintiff or the defendant has no real prospect of succeeding and there is no other compelling reason for a trial; **Order XV-A case management hearing** — the court fixes a timetable for the trial within six months; Order XVIII Rule 2 written arguments; Order XIX Rule 4-6 affidavits; Order XX Rule 1 judgment within ninety days of arguments; Sec 35 costs follow the event, with a list of factors; Sec 12A (2018) **pre-institution mediation** — a suit that does not contemplate urgent interim relief shall not be instituted unless the plaintiff has exhausted the remedy of pre-institution mediation under the rules, which the Supreme Court in Patil Automation v. Rakheja Engineers (2022) held to be mandatory, with a plaint filed in breach liable to rejection under Order VII Rule 11; appeals to the Commercial Appellate Division within sixty days (Sec 13), and no revision or Art 227 petition against interlocutory orders except through an appeal from the listed orders (Sec 8, 13)."));
c.push(H3("Section 89 and alternative dispute resolution"));
c.push(P("**Sec 89(1):** where it appears to the court that there exist elements of a settlement which may be acceptable to the parties, the court shall formulate the terms of settlement and give them to the parties for their observations and after receiving the observations may reformulate the terms and refer the same for (a) arbitration, (b) conciliation, (c) judicial settlement including settlement through Lok Adalat, or (d) mediation. **Sec 89(2):** where a dispute is referred for arbitration or conciliation the Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996 applies as if the proceedings were referred under that Act; to Lok Adalat, Sec 20(1) of the Legal Services Authorities Act 1987 applies; for judicial settlement, the court refers to a suitable institution or person deemed a Lok Adalat; for mediation, the court effects a compromise between the parties and follows the procedure prescribed. **Order X Rules 1A-1C** implement it at the first hearing. **Afcons Infrastructure v. Cherian Varkey Construction (2010)** corrected the drafting: the definitions of 'mediation' and 'judicial settlement' in Sec 89(2)(c)-(d) are interchanged and should be read the right way round; the court need not formulate terms in detail but only describe the dispute briefly; arbitration and conciliation require consent (a reference without consent is a nullity — Jagdish Chander v. Ramesh Chander, 2007; Salem (II)); the other three modes do not; the court should consider Sec 89 after pleadings, and a reference to mediation should be attempted in almost every suitable case; the categories of suitable and unsuitable cases. **Salem Advocate Bar Association (II)** approved the Model Mediation Rules, directed High Courts to frame rules and establish mediation centres, and held that when a case is settled under Sec 89 the court fee is refunded (Sec 16 of the Court Fees Act, 1999). The 1996 Act's Part III conciliation, the Legal Services Authorities Act's Lok Adalats (Sec 19-22 — awards are deemed decrees, final and not appealable; Sec 22B permanent Lok Adalats for public-utility services, with the power to decide on merits if conciliation fails, upheld in Bar Council of India v. Union of India, 2012, subject to a monetary limit of one crore), and **the Mediation Act 2023** (in force in stages from October 2023 — mediation agreements, institutional mediation, the Mediation Council of India, court-annexed mediation, a mediated settlement agreement enforceable as a decree under Sec 27, online mediation under Sec 30, community mediation, and a time limit of one hundred and twenty days extendable by sixty; pre-litigation mediation is voluntary under Sec 5 except for commercial disputes under Sec 12A of the 2015 Act) complete the ADR structure. Court-annexed mediation centres (the Delhi 'Samadhan' centre, the Bangalore Mediation Centre, the Hyderabad and Amaravati centres) settle a significant share of matrimonial, motor accident, partition and cheque-bounce matters; the Supreme Court in M.R. Krishna Murthi v. New India Assurance (2019) recommended a Motor Accident Mediation Authority, and in Vikram Bakshi v. Sonia Khosla (2014) held that the court's role under Sec 89 includes persuasion."));
c.push(H3("Summary suits — Order XXXVII"));
c.push(P("A special fast track that predates Sec 89: Order XXXVII applies to High Courts, City Civil Courts, Courts of Small Causes and other courts notified, to suits upon bills of exchange, hundies and promissory notes, and to suits in which the plaintiff seeks only to recover a debt or liquidated demand in money payable on a written contract, or on an enactment where the sum sought to be recovered is a fixed sum of money or a debt other than a penalty, or on a guarantee where the claim against the principal is in respect of a debt or liquidated demand only (Rule 1(2), 1976). Rule 2: the suit is instituted by a plaint with a specific averment that it is under Order XXXVII, that no relief outside the Order is claimed, and with the inscription 'Under Order XXXVII'; the summons is in Form 4 of Appendix B; the defendant is not entitled to defend unless he enters an appearance within ten days of service and, after the plaintiff serves a summons for judgment, obtains **leave to defend** within ten days (Rule 3); leave is granted unconditionally where the defendant discloses facts that may be deemed sufficient to entitle him to defend, and the court may grant leave on conditions — deposit of the amount or security — where the defence is plausible but improbable; if the defendant does not apply for leave or leave is refused, the plaintiff is entitled to judgment forthwith (Rule 3(6)); Rule 4: the court may set aside a decree passed in a summary suit and stay execution for special circumstances. The tests for leave to defend were settled in Mechelec Engineers v. Basic Equipment Corporation (1977) and restated in IDBI Trusteeship Services v. Hubtown Ltd. (2016): if the defendant raises a substantial defence, unconditional leave; if the defence is fair and reasonable but raises triable issues, leave with or without conditions; if the defence is plausible but improbable, conditional leave with deposit; if there is no substantial defence, or the defence is sham, refuse leave, or grant it on deposit of the full amount; Sunil Enterprises v. SBI Commercial and International Bank (1998); Santosh Kumar v. Bhai Mool Singh (1958) — the object is to prevent unreasonable obstruction by a defendant who has no defence. The Order XIII-A summary judgment of the 2015 Act is the modern, wider cousin: available in any commercial suit, at any stage after summons but before issues, on the 'no real prospect of success' test, and for either party."));
c.push(H3("Case management, technology and the future"));
c.push(P("The Code's newest instruments are managerial rather than adversarial. **Case management (Order XV-A in commercial suits; the Civil Procedure Rules of several High Courts):** the court fixes at the first hearing a timetable for disclosure, inspection, admission, framing of issues, evidence and arguments, and the timetable may be varied only for reasons recorded; costs follow non-compliance. **Time limits** run through the amended Code: summons within thirty days (Sec 27), written statement within thirty to ninety days (Order VIII Rule 1), issues within fifteen days after the written statement? (Order XIV Rule 1(5) — at the first hearing), three adjournments (Order XVII Rule 1), day-to-day hearing, judgment within thirty to sixty days (Order XX Rule 1), the decree within fifteen days (Order XX Rule 6A), appeals to be heard within sixty days of admission (Order XLI Rule 11A) — many are directory, but together they define the target. **E-filing and virtual hearings:** the e-Courts Mission Mode Project (Phase I 2007-2015, Phase II 2015-2023, Phase III from 2023 with a budget of over seven thousand crore rupees) has computerised every district court; the Supreme Court's e-Committee's e-filing 3.0, the Case Information System, the National Judicial Data Grid, the Virtual Courts for traffic and petty cases, and the 2020 Model Rules for Video Conferencing; the Supreme Court in Swapnil Tripathi v. Supreme Court of India (2018) allowed live-streaming, and in In re: Guidelines for Court Functioning through Video Conferencing (2020) held virtual hearings to be valid 'open court' under Art 21 and Sec 153B CPC. **Electronic evidence** in civil suits: Order XI in commercial suits requires disclosure of electronic records with their source, and the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023 (Sec 61-63) governs their proof. **Costs:** Salem (II) and the 2015 Act have moved towards actual costs to deter frivolous litigation; the Law Commission's 240th Report (2012) recommended a costs regime for all suits. **The pending reform agenda:** the Law Commission's 253rd Report (Commercial Division), the Justice Malimath and the Arrears Committee proposals to reduce the tiers of appeal, the proposal to abolish second appeals altogether (the 2002 amendment's Sec 102 was a step), the Mediation Act's pre-litigation mediation, the Legislative Department's draft Code of Civil Procedure (Amendment) Bill 2023? — the trend is clear: fewer hearings, earlier settlement, paper replaced by data, and the trial reserved for cases that genuinely need it. The critique: the Bar's concern (Salem I) that time limits without more judges shift the burden to litigants; the empirical finding that adjournments continue because the amendments are read as directory; and the access-to-justice worry that e-filing and mediation may disadvantage the poor litigant whom Order XXXIII and Sec 91 were designed to protect."));
c.push(CASE("Afcons Infrastructure Ltd. v. Cherian Varkey Construction Co. (P) Ltd. (2010) 8 SCC 24","Sec 89 read purposively: five ADR modes; arbitration and conciliation need consent, judicial settlement, Lok Adalat and mediation do not; the court need not formulate detailed terms; lists of suitable and unsuitable cases; the definitions in Sec 89(2)(c) and (d) are to be read as interchanged."));
c.push(CASE("Salem Advocate Bar Association v. Union of India (II) (2005) 6 SCC 344","Upheld and explained the 1999-2002 amendments; approved Model Mediation Rules; directed refund of court fee on settlement; time limits are to be applied purposively."));
c.push(CASE("Patil Automation Pvt. Ltd. v. Rakheja Engineers Pvt. Ltd. (2022) 10 SCC 1","Pre-institution mediation under Sec 12A of the Commercial Courts Act is mandatory; a suit filed without exhausting it, where no urgent interim relief is sought, is liable to be rejected under Order VII Rule 11."));
c.push(CASE("IDBI Trusteeship Services Ltd. v. Hubtown Ltd. (2017) 1 SCC 568","Restated the principles for leave to defend in summary suits under Order XXXVII Rule 3: unconditional leave for a substantial defence, conditional leave for a plausible but improbable defence, and refusal where the defence is sham."));
c.push(CASE("Swapnil Tripathi v. Supreme Court of India (2018) 10 SCC 639","Live-streaming of court proceedings is an extension of the open-court principle under Art 21 and Art 145(4); guidelines framed."));
c.push(DIG("The whole of this episode is digital: online dispute resolution platforms (the Supreme Court's e-Committee ODR framework, NITI Aayog's 2021 ODR report, private platforms like Sama and Presolv360) handle small-value disputes; Lok Adalats have been held online ('e-Lok Adalats') since 2020 settling lakhs of cases; the Mediation Act 2023 expressly permits online mediation (Sec 30) and treats the electronically signed settlement as the agreement; the e-Courts Phase III plan funds 'digital courts' with AI-assisted transcription (the Supreme Court's SUVAS translation and SUPACE research tools) and a unified e-filing system for all courts; and the NJDG's public dashboards make adjournment counts and pendency visible district by district. The examiner's angle is comparative: how do these tools relate to the Code's existing rules (Order V Rule 9 service, Order XVIII Rule 4 affidavits, Order XXVI commissions, Sec 89 references) and what safeguards do the Salem and Afcons principles require."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Sec 89', 'Lok Adalat', 'Mediation', 'Summary suit', 'Leave to defend', 'Pre-institution mediation', 'Commercial Courts Act', 'E-filing'. Part B: 'Discuss the scope of Sec 89 in the light of Afcons and the changes made by the 1999 and 2002 amendments to speed up civil trials' — Sec 89 text, Order X Rule 1A, Afcons's five modes and consent rule, Salem (II) on mediation rules and court fee, then the time-limit amendments; or 'Explain the procedure in summary suits under Order XXXVII and the principles governing leave to defend'. Part C: a court refers a partition suit to arbitration over one party's objection — nullity (Afcons, Jagdish Chander); a commercial suit filed without pre-institution mediation and without urgent relief — reject (Patil Automation); a defendant in a summary suit on a promissory note pleads that the note was a security for a different transaction — a triable issue, conditional leave."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- DRILL BANK ----------
c.push(H1("Part C Drill Bank — 10 Problems with IRAC Answers"));
c.push(P("Write each answer in four moves: Issue (one line), Rule (section, Order and Rule, case), Application (the facts against the rule), Conclusion (the result and the remedy)."));
const drills=[
["1. Two suits on one agreement","A sues B for an injunction restraining B from selling a plot that B agreed to sell to A. A loses. A then sues B for specific performance of the same agreement without having obtained leave in the first suit.","Issue: is the second suit barred? Rule: Order II Rule 2(3) — a plaintiff entitled to more than one relief on the same cause of action who omits to sue for all without leave cannot afterwards sue for the omitted relief (Gurbux Singh; Virgo Industries). Application: same agreement, same bundle of facts, injunction and specific performance are two reliefs on it, no leave. Conclusion: the second suit is barred; B must produce the earlier plaint to prove it."],
["2. A stale claim in a second round","A tenant's suit for injunction against eviction is dismissed after trial. He then sues for a declaration that he is the owner by adverse possession, on facts he knew during the first suit.","Issue: res judicata. Rule: Sec 11 Explanation IV — constructive res judicata bars a ground of attack that might and ought to have been raised (State of U.P. v. Nawab Hussain). Application: ownership by adverse possession was available and ought to have been pleaded in the first suit between the same parties under the same title. Conclusion: the second suit is barred."],
["3. Plaint filed on the last day but in the wrong court","A files a suit in the District Court at Guntur on the last day of limitation; after two years the court returns the plaint under Order VII Rule 10 for want of territorial jurisdiction and A presents it at Vijayawada the next week.","Issue: is the re-presented suit within time? Rule: Order VII Rule 10 return; Sec 14(1) Limitation Act excludes the time spent prosecuting with due diligence and in good faith in a court unable to entertain the suit for defect of jurisdiction (Consolidated Engineering conditions). Application: same matter, same parties, civil proceeding, bona fide filing, defect of jurisdiction. Conclusion: the two years are excluded; the suit is within time (subject to proof of good faith)."],
["4. Written statement on the one hundred and tenth day","In an ordinary money suit the defendant files his written statement one hundred and ten days after service, explaining that he was hospitalised for six weeks.","Issue: can the court receive it? Rule: Order VIII Rule 1 — thirty days, extendable to ninety; Kailash v. Nanhku and Salem (II) — the limit is directory, and in exceptional cases the court may extend on costs with reasons; contrast SCG Contracts for commercial suits. Application: an ordinary suit, a genuine medical reason. Conclusion: the court may take the written statement on record on costs, recording reasons; in a commercial suit it would be forfeited."],
["5. Ex parte injunction without reasons","The Munsif grants an ex parte temporary injunction restraining the defendant from constructing, without recording why notice was dispensed with, and does not decide the application for forty-five days.","Issue: validity of the order and remedy. Rule: Order XXXIX Rule 3 proviso — reasons for dispensing with notice are mandatory (Shiv Kumar Chadha); Rule 3A — endeavour to decide within thirty days; Rule 4 — application to vacate; Order XLIII Rule 1(r) — appeal. Application: no reasons, no compliance, delay beyond thirty days. Conclusion: the order is liable to be vacated on an application under Rule 4 or set aside in appeal; the defendant may also appeal the failure to decide within thirty days (Venkatasubbiah Naidu)."],
["6. Arrest for a debt","A decree-holder applies for the arrest of the judgment-debtor, a daily-wage worker, for a decree of eight thousand rupees. The court issues a warrant without notice or inquiry.","Issue: legality of the arrest. Rule: Sec 51 proviso — detention only on a finding, after opportunity, of means and refusal, dishonest transfer, or likelihood of absconding (Jolly George Varghese); Order XXI Rule 37 — show-cause notice first; Rule 40 — hearing. Application: no notice, no inquiry into means, a poor debtor. Conclusion: the warrant is illegal; the court must issue notice and, unless one of the grounds is proved, cannot detain him."],
["7. Suit against the State without notice","A supplier files a suit against the State of Andhra Pradesh for the price of goods and for an urgent injunction restraining the encashment of his bank guarantee, without serving a Sec 80 notice and without seeking leave.","Issue: maintainability. Rule: Sec 80(1) notice mandatory (Bihari Chowdhary); Sec 80(2) — a suit for urgent relief may be filed with the leave of the court, and the court returns the plaint if no urgent relief is needed (Pioneer Builders). Application: urgency exists as to the bank guarantee, but leave was not sought. Conclusion: the suit as filed is not maintainable; the plaintiff must apply for leave under Sec 80(2), failing which the plaint is returned after two months' notice."],
["8. Compromise for a minor","In a partition suit the next friend of a minor plaintiff enters into a compromise giving the minor a smaller share; the court records it without any application for leave. The minor, on attaining majority, sues to set aside the decree.","Issue: validity of the compromise and the remedy. Rule: Order XXXII Rule 7 — no compromise on behalf of a minor without the leave of the court expressly recorded; without leave it is voidable against all parties other than the minor (Ram Chandra Arya); Order XXIII Rule 3A bars a suit to set aside a compromise decree, but the minor's remedy is under Rule 7(2) and Rule 12. Application: no leave, prejudice to the minor. Conclusion: the compromise is void as against the minor; he may avoid it by application in the same suit; the separate suit is not the proper route."],
["9. Second appeal to reappreciate evidence","The first appellate court reverses the trial court's finding on possession after considering the evidence. The High Court admits a second appeal 'to examine whether the finding is correct' and restores the trial court's decree.","Issue: scope of Sec 100. Rule: a second appeal lies only on a substantial question of law formulated at admission (Sec 100(3)-(5); Santosh Hazari; Kondiba; Gurdev Kaur); a finding of fact is open only if perverse or based on no evidence (Hero Vinoth). Application: no question formulated; pure reappreciation of evidence. Conclusion: the High Court's judgment is without jurisdiction and liable to be set aside; the appeal should be reheard on a properly formulated question, if any."],
["10. Delay of one hundred days in an appeal","A litigant files a first appeal one hundred days late, stating that his advocate had told him that the certified copy would be applied for and did not do so; the certified copy was in fact applied for and obtained within a week of the judgment.","Issue: condonation under Sec 5. Rule: 'sufficient cause' is liberal (Katiji) but requires bona fides and diligence (Balakrishnan; Basawaraj; Esha Bhattacharjee); the mistake of counsel may be sufficient if the litigant was not negligent (Rafiq v. Munshilal; Salil Dutta); Sec 12(2) excludes only the time requisite for the copy. Application: the copy was ready early, so Sec 12 gives little; the explanation blames counsel without showing the litigant's own diligence for one hundred days. Conclusion: condonation is doubtful; the court may condone on costs if the litigant's affidavit shows he acted in good faith and followed up, otherwise the appeal is dismissed as barred."]
];
drills.forEach(([t,f,a])=>{c.push(H3(t));c.push(P("**Facts:** "+f));c.push(P(a));});
c.push(PB());

